{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-bbaa307786ee661d", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\t                                         Modeling Stylistic Co-evolution in Symbolic Music Heritage Collections\n2\t\n3\t                                         YULONG HE, St. Petersburg State University, Russia\n4\t                                         IVAN SMIRNOV, ITMO University, Russia\n5\t                                         YANMING LI, St. Petersburg State Institute of Culture, Russia\n6\t                                         Digitized musical heritage collections offer new opportunities to examine how stylistic traditions change over historical time, but\n7\t                                         computational analyses often reduce musical works to static classifications or similarity scores. This article proposes a representation-\n8\t                                         to-dynamics framework for studying cross-cultural harmonic change in Western art music. Starting from symbolic chord sequences, we\n9\t                                         derive contextual chord embeddings, project work-level representations into a shared harmonic space, and reconstruct country-level\n10\t                                         trajectories through temporally causal Kalman filtering. These trajectories are then modeled with DeGroot and Friedkin–Johnsen\n11\t                                         dynamics, yielding interpretable influence-like networks and estimates of stylistic anchoring. We apply the framework to a curated\n12\t                                         corpus of 480 dated works from 1875 to 1940 across Russia, France, Germany, Austria, and a heterogeneous ”Others” group. Models\n13\t                                         fitted on 1875–1925 are evaluated through recursive forecasts for 1930–1940, testing whether the estimated dependency structure\n14\t                                         remains informative across a potentially changing historical and stylistic regime. The estimated trajectories and influence patterns are\n15\t                                         broadly consistent with established music-historical accounts of late Romantic and early modernist exchange, including the close\n16\tarXiv:2607.23957v1 [cs.SD] 27 Jul 2026\n17\t\n18\t\n19\t\n20\t\n21\t                                         relationship between German and Austrian traditions and historically plausible cross-currents between Russian and French traditions.\n22\t                                         PCA-variance-weighted estimation provides modest improvements while preserving a single interpretable influence network. Rather\n23\t                                         than treating the estimated matrices as direct causal evidence, the framework offers a reproducible quantitative layer for cultural\n24\t                                         heritage research, complementing archival and musicological interpretation.\n25\t\n26\t                                         CCS Concepts: • Applied computing → Arts and humanities; Sound and music computing; • Computing methodologies →\n27\t                                         Modeling methodologies; Machine learning; • Mathematics of computing → Time series analysis.\n28\t\n29\t\n30\t\n31\t                                         1    Introduction\n32\t                                         Musical style evolution offers a structured and quantifiable window into the mechanisms of cultural transmission.\n33\t                                         Across historical epochs, aesthetic traditions emerge through the interplay of endogenous creative trajectories and\n34\t                                         exogenous cross-border influences. Although musicologists have extensively documented these processes through\n35\t                                         qualitative scholarship, computational models that characterize stylistic diffusion across national traditions remain\n36\t                                         underdeveloped.\n37\t                                             Current research exhibits a methodological divide. In Music Information Retrieval (MIR), advances in representation\n38\t                                         learning and style classification typically treat musical works as static objects or focus on univariate period discrimina-\n39\t                                         tion [22, 36]. Conversely, opinion dynamics models in computational social science provide rigorous frameworks for\n40\t                                         belief propagation and consensus formation [4, 6], but lack culturally grounded, high-dimensional observables. Bridging\n41\t                                         these domains requires (i) a validated proxy for national stylistic “opinion,” (ii) a robust signal extraction pipeline to\n42\t                                         handle sparse and noisy historical observations, and (iii) a dynamical model that explicitly disentangles endogenous\n43\t                                         tradition from exogenous influence.\n44\t                                             To address this gap, we present a unified computational framework (See Fig. 1) that integrates harmonic representation\n45\t                                         learning, signal processing, and opinion dynamics modeling. Using symbolic works from 1875–1940, a period spanning\n46\t                                         late Romanticism, musical nationalism, and early modernism, we construct a representation-to-dynamics framework\n47\t\n48\t                                         Authors’ Contact Information: Yulong He, St. Petersburg State University, St. Petersburg, Russia; Ivan Smirnov, ITMO University, St. Petersburg, Russia;\n49\t                                         Yanming Li, St. Petersburg State Institute of Culture, St. Petersburg, Russia.\n50\t                                                                                                                                                                                               1\n51\t\f2                                                                                                                He et al.\n52\t\n53\t\n54\t\n55\t\n56\t                                         Fig. 1. Overview of the proposed framework.\n57\t\n58\t\n59\t\n60\tfor analyzing long-term stylistic change in digitized musical heritage collections. The dynamical models are fitted on\n61\t1875–1925 and evaluated retrospectively through recursive forecasts for 1930–1940.\n62\t    Specifically, we (1) learn contextual chord embeddings to capture work-level harmonic syntax, (2) formulate cultural\n63\tevolution as a latent signal recovery problem and apply filtering theory to denoise sparse temporal observations,\n64\tand (3) formulate stylistic evolution as opinion dynamics, enabling interpretable modeling of cross-cultural harmonic\n65\tdependencies and stylistic persistence. This enables quantitative estimation of asymmetric stylistic dependencies,\n66\tincluding the close stylistic connection observed between German and Austrian traditions.\n67\tContributions. Our key contributions are as follows. First, we introduce a representation-to-dynamics framework that\n68\tlinks work-level harmonic embeddings, temporally filtered cultural trajectories, and interpretable opinion-dynamics\n69\tmodels for studying long-term stylistic co-evolution in symbolic music heritage collections.\n70\t    Second, we develop and evaluate leakage-free harmonic representations for symbolic musical works by training chord\n71\tembedding models exclusively on external chord corpora, while keeping the historical target corpus fully separated\n72\tfrom representation learning and model selection.\n73\t    Third, we demonstrate the framework on a curated symbolic music corpus with source-backed work-level temporal\n74\tannotations. The main analysis focuses on 480 works from 1875–1940 across five cultural groups (Russia, France, Ger-\n75\tmany, Austria, and Others), and examines how sparse observations, heterogeneous aggregation, and model assumptions\n76\taffect the reconstructed stylistic trajectories.\n77\t    Finally, we provide diagnostic extensions and open-source resources, a MIDI-based stress test, and released embed-\n78\tdings, signal extraction code, and dynamics estimation scripts to support replication and future research in computational\n79\tcultural analysis.\n80\t\fModeling Stylistic Co-evolution in Symbolic Music Heritage Collections                                                    3\n81\t\n82\t\n83\t    The remainder of this paper is organized as follows: §2 reviews related work; §3–§5.1 detail dataset construction,\n84\trepresentation learning, and opinion dynamics analysis; and §6 discuss empirical findings, limitations, and broader\n85\timplications. The appendices (§B– §C) provide supplementary diagnostics, including a MIDI-based stress test. The code\n86\tis available at https://github.com/hreyulog/music_opinion_dynamic.\n87\t\n88\t2     Related Work\n89\tOur work intersects four active research areas: computational musicology, representation learning for symbolic music,\n90\topinion dynamics modeling, and cross-cultural influence analysis. We position our contributions at the intersection of\n91\tthese domains.\n92\t\n93\t2.1    Computational Musicology, Symbolic Representation, and Cultural Influence\n94\tThe quantitative analysis of musical style has a long tradition in musicology [1, 3]. Early computational approaches often\n95\trelied on rule-based or hand-crafted musical features for tasks such as period classification and stylistic comparison [17].\n96\tMore recent work has used large-scale symbolic and audio corpora to study stylistic change as a continuous historical\n97\tprocess rather than as a set of discrete period labels [22, 36]. For example, Mauch et al. [22] analyze harmonic and\n98\ttimbral evolution in the Billboard corpus from 1960 to 2010, identifying patterns of “punctuated equilibria” in popular\n99\tmusic. Weiss et al. [36] extend this line of inquiry to classical music by using tonal complexity metrics to track stylistic\n100\ttrajectories across composer lifetimes.\n101\t    In parallel, representation learning for symbolic music has become an important approach for modeling musical\n102\tstructure beyond manually designed features. Early approaches represented chord or note sequences with bag-of-\n103\twords-style features [37], while more recent methods adapt techniques from natural language processing, including\n104\tWord2Vec [23] and Transformer architectures [32], to musical sequences. In the harmonic domain, Chord2Vec [19] learns\n105\tdistributed chord embeddings through Skip-gram training and demonstrates their usefulness for style classification and\n106\tretrieval. These studies provide important foundations for large-scale musical style analysis, but most existing work\n107\teither focuses on static representation, classification, or univariate temporal trends.\n108\t    A related line of work in digital humanities and cultural analytics studies cultural influence across geographic,\n109\tinstitutional, and social boundaries. Network-based approaches have been applied to literary translation flows [24], art\n110\tauction prices [33], and scientific collaboration [25]. In music, prior work has mapped composer influence through\n111\tbiographical networks [28] or stylistic similarity [29]. Such approaches are valuable for describing relationships among\n112\tcultural actors, but they often infer influence post hoc from observed links or similarities, rather than modeling how\n113\tstylistic trajectories evolve over time.\n114\t    Our work builds on this literature by combining symbolic music representation learning with interpretable dynamical\n115\tmodeling. We compare a Skip-gram chord embedding baseline with Transformer-based chord representations and use\n116\tthe resulting work-level harmonic embeddings as observables for long-term stylistic trajectories. Instead of treating\n117\tcross-cultural relationships as static similarities, we estimate influence-like dependency matrices that best explain\n118\tthe observed evolution of country-level harmonic states under opinion-dynamics models. This enables a cautious,\n119\tmodel-based analysis of cross-cultural stylistic co-evolution in historical musical heritage data.\n120\t\n121\t2.2    Opinion Dynamics for Cultural Trajectories\n122\tOpinion dynamics models provide a mathematical language for describing how individual or collective states evolve\n123\tthrough interaction. Originating in sociology, social psychology, statistical physics and network science, these models\n124\t\f4                                                                                                               He et al.\n125\t\n126\t\n127\tare commonly used to study belief formation, consensus, polarization, susceptibility, and persistence in interconnected\n128\tpopulations [4, 6]. In the classical DeGroot model (DG model) [4], each agent updates its state (often referred to as\n129\tan “opinion” in the social influence literature) by taking a weighted average of the current states of other agents. In\n130\tour setting, the “agents” are cultural groups rather than individuals, and the “opinions” are not subjective beliefs but\n131\tlatent harmonic-style states derived from symbolic musical representations. The influence structure is encoded by\n132\ta row-stochastic matrix whose rows describe how strongly each agent depends on others. This linear formulation\n133\tremains useful because it connects the observed temporal trajectories to an interpretable latent interaction structure.\n134\t    The Friedkin-Johnsen (FJ) model extends dynamics by allowing each agent to remain partially attached to an intrinsic\n135\tor initial opinion [6]. Opinion change is therefore governed by both social influence and anchoring to an internal\n136\tpreference or prior state. The resulting susceptibility or stubbornness parameters are particularly relevant in settings\n137\twhere agents do not simply converge through repeated averaging, but instead retain persistent identities, traditions, or\n138\tinstitutional constraints.\n139\t    Although these models are often introduced for scalar opinions, later work has generalized opinion dynamics to\n140\tvector-valued or multidimensional states, where each agent holds opinions across multiple dimensions [7, 26, 27]. This\n141\tperspective is important for cultural and musical data, where stylistic variation cannot be adequately represented by a\n142\tsingle scalar value. Related PCA-based approaches have constructed scalar opinion trajectories from a single principal\n143\tcomponent for the evolution of code repositories [15]. In contrast, our framework retains multiple PCA coordinates and\n144\trepresents each cultural group by a vector-valued harmonic state. The resulting country-by-component matrix X(𝑡)\n145\tallows DG and FJ models to operate on multidimensional musical trajectories while still estimating a single interpretable\n146\tcross-group dependency matrix.\n147\t    Beyond their original use for human opinions, opinion-dynamics models have increasingly been adapted as inter-\n148\tpretable tools for evolving traces of behavior, language, sentiment, and representation. Recent studies apply these\n149\tmodels to real-world digital traces such as software repositories and social-media trajectories, where high-dimensional\n150\tobservations are first transformed into lower-dimensional temporal states and then modeled as interacting processes [14–\n151\t16]. These applications suggest that opinion dynamics can function as a general framework for modeling temporal\n152\tdependence among latent states.\n153\t    Applying opinion dynamics to historical music requires a change in the definition of the agent. In standard opinion-\n154\tdynamics settings, agents are usually assumed to coexist within the same time window and to update their states\n155\tthrough contemporaneous interaction. Individual composers do not satisfy this assumption in a long-horizon historical\n156\tcorpus: their lifetimes, active creative periods, and surviving works are unevenly distributed, and many composers\n157\tnever overlap temporally with one another. Treating composers as individual agents would, therefore, produce a highly\n158\tsparse and discontinuous interaction system. For this reason, we define the agents at the level of national or cultural\n159\tgroups. These groups should not be interpreted as literal decision-making actors, but as analytical units that make it\n160\tpossible to aggregate works into continuous harmonic-style trajectories over multiple decades. This aggregation allows\n161\tthe DG and FJ models to be used in a restricted, descriptive sense: the estimated matrices summarize influence-like\n162\tdependencies among group-level stylistic trajectories. This distinction is important for interpreting the model. The\n163\tresulting dynamics do not claim that countries themselves form opinions or that cultural influence can be reduced to\n164\tnational interaction. Rather, country and cultural-group labels provide a practical temporal scaffold for studying how\n165\tharmonic features associated with different traditions co-evolve over time. Contextual chord representations supply\n166\tthe musical evidence, Kalman filtering converts sparse work-level observations into temporally ordered group-level\n167\t\fModeling Stylistic Co-evolution in Symbolic Music Heritage Collections                                                 5\n168\t\n169\t\n170\t\n171\t\n172\t                                 Fig. 2. Distribution of musical works by country over time (1654–1940).\n173\t\n174\t\n175\t\n176\tstates, and opinion-dynamics models provide an interpretable summary of self-persistence, cross-group dependence,\n177\tand anchoring in these long-term trajectories.\n178\t\n179\t3     Dataset\n180\t3.1    Cross-Era Dataset\n181\tWe conduct our historical analysis on the Cross-Era dataset [21, 34–38], a collection of symbolic music annotations\n182\tcovering multiple historical periods. The dataset provides chord sequences aligned with musical works, along with\n183\tmetadata such as composer identity and nationality.\n184\t    The Cross-Era dataset comprises 2,000 musical works by 70 composers. Each work is represented as a sequence of\n185\tchord labels obtained via automated chord recognition at regular temporal intervals. These chord sequences form the\n186\tbasis for constructing harmonic representations and country-level musical trajectories in the subsequent analysis.\n187\t    While composer-level metadata is readily available, work-level creation years are often incomplete, inconsistent, or\n188\tabsent from the original metadata. To enable fine-grained temporal analysis, we construct an auxiliary set of work-level\n189\tyear annotations by collecting and cross-validating information from multiple source-backed musicological resources.\n190\tThese sources include IMSLP work pages and catalogues, Wikidata item properties, Wikipedia work pages and composer\n191\twork lists, RISM catalogue records, orchestra and concert-program notes, publisher catalogues, and specialized music\n192\treference databases.\n193\t    For each matched work, we record the inferred composition year or year range, the supporting sources, a confidence\n194\tlabel, and notes describing ambiguous cases, source conflicts, or cases where only a publication, premiere, or manuscript\n195\tdate is available. The resulting annotations are released as a supplementary dataset on Hugging Face1 . This source-\n196\tbacked annotation process provides work-level temporal information for individual compositions, allowing us to\n197\tconduct temporal analysis at the level of individual works rather than relying solely on aggregated composer-level\n198\t\n199\t1 Available at https://huggingface.co/datasets/StravynDynamics/cross-era-classical-work-years\n200\t\f6                                                                                                               He et al.\n201\t\n202\t\n203\ttimelines. Figure 2 illustrates the distribution of works across 13 countries over the extended temporal range. The\n204\tstacked bar chart shows the number of works per 5-year bin, while the line plot highlights temporal trends for individual\n205\tcountries.\n206\t    Based on this distribution, we restrict the subsequent opinion dynamics analysis to the period 1875–1940. This\n207\tchoice is motivated by both data coverage and music-historical considerations. First, our opinion dynamics model\n208\trequires each agent to be sufficiently represented across temporal windows, so that the inferred trajectories are not\n209\tdominated by sparse or missing observations. We therefore select the four most consistently represented national\n210\ttraditions—Germany, Austria, France, and Russia—as separate agents, while grouping the remaining countries into a\n211\tcomposite Others agent.\n212\t    Second, the period 1875–1940 corresponds to a major transitional phase from late Romanticism to musical modernism.\n213\tThe years 1875–1900 are characterized by late Romantic idioms and the flourishing of national schools, represented\n214\tby composers such as Brahms, Tchaikovsky, Dvořák, and the early Mahler. The period 1900–1918 marks a phase of\n215\tmodernist breakthroughs, including Impressionism, Expressionism, and the emergence of atonality, with figures such as\n216\tDebussy, Ravel, Schoenberg, and Stravinsky reshaping harmonic and formal language. The interwar period, 1918–1940,\n217\tfurther exhibits a pluralistic modernist landscape in which neoclassicism, twelve-tone technique, nationalist modernism,\n218\tand jazz-inflected styles coexisted.\n219\t    This time window is therefore particularly suitable for opinion dynamics analysis: it captures a historically dense\n220\ttransition in which multiple musical centers and aesthetic movements competed, interacted, and influenced one another.\n221\tGermany and Austria trace a trajectory from late Romanticism toward Expressionism, atonality, and twelve-tone\n222\torganization; France moves from Impressionism toward neoclassicism; Russia develops from nationalist traditions into\n223\tmodernist and émigré composer networks; and the Others agent captures the heterogeneous contributions of Czech,\n224\tHungarian, Italian, Nordic, American, and other national or modernist styles.\n225\t\n226\t3.2   External Chord Corpora for Representation Learning\n227\tThe Cross-Era dataset is used as the target corpus for the historical and cross-national analyses in this study. To avoid\n228\tfitting the harmonic representation directly to the target corpus, we train and select the representation model using\n229\texternal chord corpora only. This design allows the harmonic representations assigned to works in Cross-Era to be\n230\tobtained in a zero-shot, out-of-domain setting rather than being optimized on the same data used for the downstream\n231\tanalysis.\n232\t    We construct and evaluate a family of masked-language models for chord sequences, including a DeBERTa-based\n233\tvariant that we refer to as ChordBERT2 . The goal of this stage is not to tune the representation on the downstream\n234\tCross-Era corpus, but to select a work-level harmonic representation through experiments on external chord datasets.\n235\t    We use three external resources with distinct roles. First, Chordonomicon [18] is used as a large source-domain\n236\tcorpus for general chord representation learning. Although it consists primarily of contemporary and popular-music\n237\tchord progressions, its large scale provides broad coverage of chord vocabulary and common progression patterns.\n238\tSecond, the When-in-Rome functional-harmony corpus [10] is used as a classical-music domain-adaptation corpus.\n239\tBecause it contains symbolic functional-harmony annotations from the classical repertoire, it allows us to test whether\n240\tcontinued masked-language pre-training on classical harmonic material improves the learned representations. Third,\n241\tthe DCML corpora are reserved exclusively for validation and model selection. They provide a clean, expert-annotated\n242\t\n243\t2 Model available at https://huggingface.co/StravynDynamics/ChordBert\n244\t\fModeling Stylistic Co-evolution in Symbolic Music Heritage Collections                                                      7\n245\t\n246\tTable 1. Corpus statistics before and after filtering. Counts refer to progressions for Chordonomicon, works for When-in-Rome,\n247\tscores for DCML, and tracks for Cross-Era.\n248\t\n249\t                        Corpus              Role                    Before Items     Retained Items\n250\t                        Chordonomicon       Source pre-training          679807             679807\n251\t                        When-in-Rome        DAPT training                   1500                 704\n252\t                        DCML                Validation                      1283                 367\n253\t                        Cross-Era           Target analysis                 2000               2000\n254\t\n255\t\n256\t\n257\tbenchmark of classical harmonic analyses, enabling us to evaluate whether representations trained on different corpus\n258\tcombinations preserve musically meaningful harmonic structure.\n259\t    Accordingly, we compare model variants trained under different data regimes, including source-domain training on\n260\tChordonomicon, classical-domain training on When-in-Rome, and combined source-plus-domain training. All variants\n261\tare evaluated on the held-out DCML validation set, while Cross-Era is kept entirely separate from representation\n262\tlearning. This design ensures that the chord embedding model used for the downstream historical analysis is selected\n263\twithout using the target corpus for pre-training, domain adaptation, validation, model selection, or hyperparameter\n264\ttuning.\n265\t    To prevent leakage among the classical symbolic corpora, we construct mutually exclusive partitions with the\n266\tfollowing priority order: Cross-Era > DCML > When-in-Rome. The Cross-Era dataset is held out entirely and all\n267\t2,000 works are retained for the final downstream analysis. The DCML corpora are used only for validation. Works\n268\toverlapping with Cross-Era are removed from DCML using normalized composer names, catalogue identifiers such as\n269\tBWV, K., Op., and WoO numbers, and fuzzy title matching. Ambiguous Mozart piano sonatas without reliable Köchel\n270\tnumbers are conservatively removed. This procedure yields a validation set of 367 works.\n271\t    The classical domain-adaptation partition is constructed from the When-in-Rome functional-harmony corpus. Works\n272\toverlapping with Cross-Era are removed. In addition, all composers represented in the final DCML validation set\n273\tare held out from this training partition, preventing composer-level leakage between domain-adaptive training and\n274\tvalidation. The resulting When-in-Rome training set contains 704 works.\n275\t    Table 1 summarizes the corpus partitioning used in the representation-learning pipeline. Chordonomicon provides\n276\tthe large-scale contemporary/popular-music source domain, When-in-Rome provides the classical domain-adaptation\n277\tpartition, DCML is reserved for classical validation and model selection, and Cross-Era is retained in full as the held-out\n278\ttarget corpus for the downstream historical analysis.\n279\t\n280\t\n281\t4     Chord Representation Learning\n282\t4.1    Chord Vocabulary Normalization\n283\tAll corpora are converted to the chord notation expected by the original ChordBERT tokenizer, a word-level whitespace\n284\ttokenizer in which each chord and each special label (e.g., <chorus_3>) are treated as a single token, resulting in a\n285\tvocabulary of 3,230 tokens (obtained after training on the Chordonomicon dataset’s note sequences). Representative\n286\tconversions include C_maj → C, F#_min → Fsmin, A_maj_min7 → A7, and C_min_min7 → Cmin7.\n287\t    Some source chord labels are not covered by the tokenizer vocabulary. We rewrite these labels into supported\n288\tchord-token forms using fixed conversion rules, while preserving the original labels in the metadata. No-chord events\n289\t(conventionally denoted N [12]) are retained in the raw records but are excluded during conversion, as they have no\n290\t\f8                                                                                                                     He et al.\n291\t\n292\t\n293\tcorresponding token in the ChordBERT vocabulary. We verified that all exported sequences consist exclusively of\n294\tvalid vocabulary tokens.\n295\t\n296\t\n297\t4.2    Chord Representation Models\n298\t4.2.1 Word2Vec-style Skip-gram Chord Embeddings. We additionally consider a Word2Vec-style Skip-gram model\n299\tfollowing the Chord2Vec approach as an embedding baseline. Let a chord sequence be denoted as (𝑐 1, 𝑐 2, . . . , 𝑐 𝐿 ), where\n300\t                                                                                           pc\n301\t𝐿 denotes the sequence length and ℓ indexes the position of a centre chord. Let 𝑥𝑟 (𝑐) ∈ {0, 1} indicate whether pitch\n302\tclass 𝑟 ∈ {1, . . . , 𝑅} is present in chord 𝑐, where 𝑅 denotes the number of pitch classes.\n303\t     Given a centre chord 𝑐 ℓ and a context chord 𝑐 ℓ+Δ , the probability of pitch class 𝑟 occurring in the context chord is\n304\tmodelled as\n305\t\n306\t\n307\t                                                       𝑝𝑟 (𝑐 ℓ ) = 𝜎 a𝑟⊤ e(𝑐 ℓ ) + 𝑏𝑟 ,\n308\t                                                                                     \u0001\n309\t\n310\t     where e(𝑐 ℓ ) denotes the learned embedding of the centre chord, a𝑟 and 𝑏𝑟 are decoder parameters associated with\n311\tpitch class 𝑟 , and 𝜎 (·) is the logistic sigmoid function.\n312\t     Assuming that pitch classes are conditionally independent given the centre chord, the Skip-gram objective is defined\n313\tas\n314\t\n315\t                                 𝐿\n316\t                                ∑︁     𝑅\n317\t                                   ∑︁ ∑︁ \u0002 pc                               pc                            \u0003\n318\t                      LSG = −             𝑥𝑟 (𝑐 ℓ+Δ ) log 𝑝𝑟 (𝑐 ℓ ) + (1 − 𝑥𝑟 (𝑐 ℓ+Δ )) log(1 − 𝑝𝑟 (𝑐 ℓ )) .                 (1)\n319\t                                ℓ=1 Δ∈ C (ℓ ) 𝑟 =1\n320\t     where\n321\t\n322\t\n323\t                                              C(ℓ) = {Δ : 1 ≤ ℓ + Δ ≤ 𝐿, 0 < |Δ| ≤ 𝑢}\n324\t     denotes the set of valid context offsets within a context window of radius 𝑢.\n325\t     After training, a fixed-size representation of a musical work 𝑚 is obtained by averaging the learned chord embeddings:\n326\t\n327\t                                                                      𝐿\n328\t                                                                      𝑚\n329\t                                                          SG       1 ∑︁\n330\t                                                         v𝑚  =           e(𝑐𝑚,ℓ ),                                           (2)\n331\t                                                                  𝐿𝑚 ℓ=1\n332\t     where 𝐿𝑚 denotes the number of chords in work 𝑚 and 𝑐𝑚,ℓ denotes the ℓ-th chord of that work.\n333\t\n334\t4.2.2 Transformer-based model. We evaluate two Transformer-based masked-language models for chord representation\n335\tlearning: a RoBERTa encoder [20] and a DeBERTa encoder [13]. Both models use the same ChordBERT tokenizer and\n336\tvocabulary, so differences in downstream performance can be attributed to the encoder architecture and training regime\n337\trather than to differences in chord normalization. The initial ChordBERT checkpoint is trained on Chordonomicon,\n338\twhich contains 679,807 contemporary chord progressions. We use 543,845 progressions for training, 67,980 for validation,\n339\tand 67,982 for testing.\n340\t     The models are trained with a masked language modelling (MLM) objective [5]. Given a chord-token sequence\n341\tc = (𝑐 1, . . . , 𝑐 𝐿 ), and a set of masked positions M, let c̃ denote the corrupted input sequence after masking the positions\n342\tin M. The MLM objective is\n343\t\n344\t                                                                 1 ∑︁\n345\t                                                     LMLM = −         log 𝑝𝜃 (𝑐 ℓ | c̃).                                     (3)\n346\t                                                                |M|\n347\t                                                                      ℓ∈M\n348\t\fModeling Stylistic Co-evolution in Symbolic Music Heritage Collections                                                             9\n349\t\n350\tTable 2. Chord- and work-level evaluation on the held-out DCML validation set. Lower is better for MLM loss; higher is better for all\n351\tother metrics. “C” denotes training on Chordonomicon, “W” denotes training on the leakage-free When-in-Rome training partition,\n352\tand “C&W” denotes training on both datasets. Dashes indicate inapplicable metrics: Chord2Vec predicts pitch-class context rather\n353\tthan masked discrete chord tokens and therefore does not support chord-level MLM evaluation.\n354\t\n355\t\n356\t                          Chord2Vec baseline                              Transformer-based model\n357\tLevel Metric                 Skip-gram                                RoBERTa                DeBERTa\n358\t                      Base        C       W      C&W Base             C        W      C&W Base              C        W      C&W\n359\t      MLM                –        –        –        –     8.1683 2.5042 6.5674 2.3951 8.1270 2.0286 6.5660 1.7906\n360\t      Top-1              –        –        –        –     0.0003 0.3476 0.0555 0.3533 0.0000 0.5326 0.1085 0.5472\n361\tChord\n362\t      Top-10             –        –        –        –     0.0044 0.8367 0.3690 0.8418 0.0003 0.8492 0.4485 0.8876\n363\t      MRR                –        –        –        –     0.0030 0.5075 0.1552 0.5173 0.0014 0.6391 0.2051 0.6645\n364\t     R@1    0.1825 0.1865 0.2024 0.1865 0.2698 0.2619 0.2540 0.2738 0.2302 0.3056 0.2381 0.2817\n365\tSame R@10   0.7540 0.7341 0.7381 0.7659 0.8016 0.7976 0.8016 0.8056 0.7937 0.8294 0.8056 0.8175\n366\twork MRR    0.3619 0.3605 0.3606 0.3624 0.4303 0.4340 0.4221 0.4441 0.4089 0.4674 0.4128 0.4548\n367\t     mAP@10 0.2049 0.1959 0.1875 0.2009 0.2582 0.2483 0.2559 0.2503 0.2544 0.2843 0.2494 0.2790\n368\t\n369\tSplit     R@1         0.3270 0.2616 0.2779 0.2970 0.5204 0.5368 0.5123 0.5395 0.4278 0.4959 0.4114 0.4768\n370\t          R@10        0.7275 0.6975 0.6812 0.7193 0.8692 0.9237 0.8692 0.9292 0.8011 0.8556 0.7956 0.8365\n371\thalf      MRR         0.4541 0.3956 0.4096 0.4211 0.6295 0.6647 0.6215 0.6686 0.5499 0.6082 0.5372 0.5940\n372\t\n373\t\n374\t   This objective encourages the encoder to infer masked chord tokens from their surrounding harmonic context.\n375\t   In addition, we apply an unsupervised SimCSE-style contrastive objective [9]. For each chord sequence in a batch,\n376\ttwo dropout-augmented representations are generated, denoted as h𝑏 and h𝑏+ . The contrastive objective is\n377\t\n378\t                                                      𝐵\n379\t                                                   1 ∑︁        exp(sim(h𝑏 , h𝑏+ )/𝜏𝑐 )\n380\t                                         LCL = −         log Í𝐵                  +\n381\t                                                                                         ,                                       (4)\n382\t                                                              𝑎=1 exp(sim(h𝑏 , h𝑎 )/𝜏𝑐 )\n383\t                                                   𝐵\n384\t                                                     𝑏=1\n385\t   where 𝐵 denotes the batch size, sim(·, ·) is cosine similarity, and 𝜏𝑐 is the temperature parameter.\n386\t   The total training objective is\n387\t\n388\t\n389\t                                                        L = LMLM + 𝜆LCL,                                                         (5)\n390\t   where 𝜆 = 0.1 controls the contribution of the contrastive objective and is fixed for all experiments.\n391\t\n392\t4.3     Domain-Adaptive Pre-training\n393\tWe trained for 3 epochs using AdamW with a batch size of 64, an initial learning rate of 5 × 10−4 , weight decay of\n394\t0.01, and gradient clipping at 1.0. A cosine learning-rate schedule with a warm-up phase covering the first 5% of\n395\toptimization steps is used. At each step, 15% of tokens are dynamically selected for masking following the standard\n396\t80/10/10 corruption strategy: 80% are replaced by [MASK], 10% by a random chord token, and 10% are left unchanged.\n397\t\n398\t4.4     Evaluation Protocol and Model Selection\n399\tWe evaluate three families of chord representation models: a non-contextual Skip-gram baseline, a RoBERTa-based\n400\tmasked-language model, and a DeBERTa-based masked-language model that we refer to as ChordBERT. The Transformer-\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "46adb53f-db2a-48fc-9bfc-1fd2b9d177eb", "uuid": "fb3ffd9a-fc56-4ce1-9b20-21bb7b510cbf", "timestamp": "2026-07-28T06:18:30.095Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_2607_23957.txt", "content": "                                         Modeling Stylistic Co-evolution in Symbolic Music Heritage Collections\n\n                                         YULONG HE, St. Petersburg State University, Russia\n                                         IVAN SMIRNOV, ITMO University, Russia\n                                         YANMING LI, St. Petersburg State Institute of Culture, Russia\n                                         Digitized musical heritage collections offer new opportunities to examine how stylistic traditions change over historical time, but\n                                         computational analyses often reduce musical works to static classifications or similarity scores. This article proposes a representation-\n                                         to-dynamics framework for studying cross-cultural harmonic change in Western art music. Starting from symbolic chord sequences, we\n                                         derive contextual chord embeddings, project work-level representations into a shared harmonic space, and reconstruct country-level\n                                         trajectories through temporally causal Kalman filtering. These trajectories are then modeled with DeGroot and Friedkin–Johnsen\n                                         dynamics, yielding interpretable influence-like networks and estimates of stylistic anchoring. We apply the framework to a curated\n                                         corpus of 480 dated works from 1875 to 1940 across Russia, France, Germany, Austria, and a heterogeneous ”Others” group. Models\n                                         fitted on 1875–1925 are evaluated through recursive forecasts for 1930–1940, testing whether the estimated dependency structure\n                                         remains informative across a potentially changing historical and stylistic regime. The estimated trajectories and influence patterns are\n                                         broadly consistent with established music-historical accounts of late Romantic and early modernist exchange, including the close\narXiv:2607.23957v1 [cs.SD] 27 Jul 2026\n\n\n\n\n                                         relationship between German and Austrian traditions and historically plausible cross-currents between Russian and French traditions.\n                                         PCA-variance-weighted estimation provides modest improvements while preserving a single interpretable influence network. Rather\n                                         than treating the estimated matrices as direct causal evidence, the framework offers a reproducible quantitative layer for cultural\n                                         heritage research, complementing archival and musicological interpretation.\n\n                                         CCS Concepts: • Applied computing → Arts and humanities; Sound and music computing; • Computing methodologies →\n                                         Modeling methodologies; Machine learning; • Mathematics of computing → Time series analysis.\n\n\n\n                                         1    Introduction\n                                         Musical style evolution offers a structured and quantifiable window into the mechanisms of cultural transmission.\n                                         Across historical epochs, aesthetic traditions emerge through the interplay of endogenous creative trajectories and\n                                         exogenous cross-border influences. Although musicologists have extensively documented these processes through\n                                         qualitative scholarship, computational models that characterize stylistic diffusion across national traditions remain\n                                         underdeveloped.\n                                             Current research exhibits a methodological divide. In Music Information Retrieval (MIR), advances in representation\n                                         learning and style classification typically treat musical works as static objects or focus on univariate period discrimina-\n                                         tion [22, 36]. Conversely, opinion dynamics models in computational social science provide rigorous frameworks for\n                                         belief propagation and consensus formation [4, 6], but lack culturally grounded, high-dimensional observables. Bridging\n                                         these domains requires (i) a validated proxy for national stylistic “opinion,” (ii) a robust signal extraction pipeline to\n                                         handle sparse and noisy historical observations, and (iii) a dynamical model that explicitly disentangles endogenous\n                                         tradition from exogenous influence.\n                                             To address this gap, we present a unified computational framework (See Fig. 1) that integrates harmonic representation\n                                         learning, signal processing, and opinion dynamics modeling. Using symbolic works from 1875–1940, a period spanning\n                                         late Romanticism, musical nationalism, and early modernism, we construct a representation-to-dynamics framework\n\n                                         Authors’ Contact Information: Yulong He, St. Petersburg State University, St. Petersburg, Russia; Ivan Smirnov, ITMO University, St. Petersburg, Russia;\n                                         Yanming Li, St. Petersburg State Institute of Culture, St. Petersburg, Russia.\n                                                                                                                                                                                               1\n\f2                                                                                                                He et al.\n\n\n\n\n                                         Fig. 1. Overview of the proposed framework.\n\n\n\nfor analyzing long-term stylistic change in digitized musical heritage collections. The dynamical models are fitted on\n1875–1925 and evaluated retrospectively through recursive forecasts for 1930–1940.\n    Specifically, we (1) learn contextual chord embeddings to capture work-level harmonic syntax, (2) formulate cultural\nevolution as a latent signal recovery problem and apply filtering theory to denoise sparse temporal observations,\nand (3) formulate stylistic evolution as opinion dynamics, enabling interpretable modeling of cross-cultural harmonic\ndependencies and stylistic persistence. This enables quantitative estimation of asymmetric stylistic dependencies,\nincluding the close stylistic connection observed between German and Austrian traditions.\nContributions. Our key contributions are as follows. First, we introduce a representation-to-dynamics framework that\nlinks work-level harmonic embeddings, temporally filtered cultural trajectories, and interpretable opinion-dynamics\nmodels for studying long-term stylistic co-evolution in symbolic music heritage collections.\n    Second, we develop and evaluate leakage-free harmonic representations for symbolic musical works by training chord\nembedding models exclusively on external chord corpora, while keeping the historical target corpus fully separated\nfrom representation learning and model selection.\n    Third, we demonstrate the framework on a curated symbolic music corpus with source-backed work-level temporal\nannotations. The main analysis focuses on 480 works from 1875–1940 across five cultural groups (Russia, France, Ger-\nmany, Austria, and Others), and examines how sparse observations, heterogeneous aggregation, and model assumptions\naffect the reconstructed stylistic trajectories.\n    Finally, we provide diagnostic extensions and open-source resources, a MIDI-based stress test, and released embed-\ndings, signal extraction code, and dynamics estimation scripts to support replication and future research in computational\ncultural analysis.\n\fModeling Stylistic Co-evolution in Symbolic Music Heritage Collections                                                    3\n\n\n    The remainder of this paper is organized as follows: §2 reviews related work; §3–§5.1 detail dataset construction,\nrepresentation learning, and opinion dynamics analysis; and §6 discuss empirical findings, limitations, and broader\nimplications. The appendices (§B– §C) provide supplementary diagnostics, including a MIDI-based stress test. The code\nis available at https://github.com/hreyulog/music_opinion_dynamic.\n\n2     Related Work\nOur work intersects four active research areas: computational musicology, representation learning for symbolic music,\nopinion dynamics modeling, and cross-cultural influence analysis. We position our contributions at the intersection of\nthese domains.\n\n2.1    Computational Musicology, Symbolic Representation, and Cultural Influence\nThe quantitative analysis of musical style has a long tradition in musicology [1, 3]. Early computational approaches often\nrelied on rule-based or hand-crafted musical features for tasks such as period classification and stylistic comparison [17].\nMore recent work has used large-scale symbolic and audio corpora to study stylistic change as a continuous historical\nprocess rather than as a set of discrete period labels [22, 36]. For example, Mauch et al. [22] analyze harmonic and\ntimbral evolution in the Billboard corpus from 1960 to 2010, identifying patterns of “punctuated equilibria” in popular\nmusic. Weiss et al. [36] extend this line of inquiry to classical music by using tonal complexity metrics to track stylistic\ntrajectories across composer lifetimes.\n    In parallel, representation learning for symbolic music has become an important approach for modeling musical\nstructure beyond manually designed features. Early approaches represented chord or note sequences with bag-of-\nwords-style features [37], while more recent methods adapt techniques from natural language processing, including\nWord2Vec [23] and Transformer architectures [32], to musical sequences. In the harmonic domain, Chord2Vec [19] learns\ndistributed chord embeddings through Skip-gram training and demonstrates their usefulness for style classification and\nretrieval. These studies provide important foundations for large-scale musical style analysis, but most existing work\neither focuses on static representation, classification, or univariate temporal trends.\n    A related line of work in digital humanities and cultural analytics studies cultural influence across geographic,\ninstitutional, and social boundaries. Network-based approaches have been applied to literary translation flows [24], art\nauction prices [33], and scientific collaboration [25]. In music, prior work has mapped composer influence through\nbiographical networks [28] or stylistic similarity [29]. Such approaches are valuable for describing relationships among\ncultural actors, but they often infer influence post hoc from observed links or similarities, rather than modeling how\nstylistic trajectories evolve over time.\n    Our work builds on this literature by combining symbolic music representation learning with interpretable dynamical\nmodeling. We compare a Skip-gram chord embedding baseline with Transformer-based chord representations and use\nthe resulting work-level harmonic embeddings as observables for long-term stylistic trajectories. Instead of treating\ncross-cultural relationships as static similarities, we estimate influence-like dependency matrices that best explain\nthe observed evolution of country-level harmonic states under opinion-dynamics models. This enables a cautious,\nmodel-based analysis of cross-cultural stylistic co-evolution in historical musical heritage data.\n\n2.2    Opinion Dynamics for Cultural Trajectories\nOpinion dynamics models provide a mathematical language for describing how individual or collective states evolve\nthrough interaction. Originating in sociology, social psychology, statistical physics and network science, these models\n\f4                                                                                                               He et al.\n\n\nare commonly used to study belief formation, consensus, polarization, susceptibility, and persistence in interconnected\npopulations [4, 6]. In the classical DeGroot model (DG model) [4], each agent updates its state (often referred to as\nan “opinion” in the social influence literature) by taking a weighted average of the current states of other agents. In\nour setting, the “agents” are cultural groups rather than individuals, and the “opinions” are not subjective beliefs but\nlatent harmonic-style states derived from symbolic musical representations. The influence structure is encoded by\na row-stochastic matrix whose rows describe how strongly each agent depends on others. This linear formulation\nremains useful because it connects the observed temporal trajectories to an interpretable latent interaction structure.\n    The Friedkin-Johnsen (FJ) model extends dynamics by allowing each agent to remain partially attached to an intrinsic\nor initial opinion [6]. Opinion change is therefore governed by both social influence and anchoring to an internal\npreference or prior state. The resulting susceptibility or stubbornness parameters are particularly relevant in settings\nwhere agents do not simply converge through repeated averaging, but instead retain persistent identities, traditions, or\ninstitutional constraints.\n    Although these models are often introduced for scalar opinions, later work has generalized opinion dynamics to\nvector-valued or multidimensional states, where each agent holds opinions across multiple dimensions [7, 26, 27]. This\nperspective is important for cultural and musical data, where stylistic variation cannot be adequately represented by a\nsingle scalar value. Related PCA-based approaches have constructed scalar opinion trajectories from a single principal\ncomponent for the evolution of code repositories [15]. In contrast, our framework retains multiple PCA coordinates and\nrepresents each cultural group by a vector-valued harmonic state. The resulting country-by-component matrix X(𝑡)\nallows DG and FJ models to operate on multidimensional musical trajectories while still estimating a single interpretable\ncross-group dependency matrix.\n    Beyond their original use for human opinions, opinion-dynamics models have increasingly been adapted as inter-\npretable tools for evolving traces of behavior, language, sentiment, and representation. Recent studies apply these\nmodels to real-world digital traces such as software repositories and social-media trajectories, where high-dimensional\nobservations are first transformed into lower-dimensional temporal states and then modeled as interacting processes [14–\n16]. These applications suggest that opinion dynamics can function as a general framework for modeling temporal\ndependence among latent states.\n    Applying opinion dynamics to historical music requires a change in the definition of the agent. In standard opinion-\ndynamics settings, agents are usually assumed to coexist within the same time window and to update their states\nthrough contemporaneous interaction. Individual composers do not satisfy this assumption in a long-horizon historical\ncorpus: their lifetimes, active creative periods, and surviving works are unevenly distributed, and many composers\nnever overlap temporally with one another. Treating composers as individual agents would, therefore, produce a highly\nsparse and discontinuous interaction system. For this reason, we define the agents at the level of national or cultural\ngroups. These groups should not be interpreted as literal decision-making actors, but as analytical units that make it\npossible to aggregate works into continuous harmonic-style trajectories over multiple decades. This aggregation allows\nthe DG and FJ models to be used in a restricted, descriptive sense: the estimated matrices summarize influence-like\ndependencies among group-level stylistic trajectories. This distinction is important for interpreting the model. The\nresulting dynamics do not claim that countries themselves form opinions or that cultural influence can be reduced to\nnational interaction. Rather, country and cultural-group labels provide a practical temporal scaffold for studying how\nharmonic features associated with different traditions co-evolve over time. Contextual chord representations supply\nthe musical evidence, Kalman filtering converts sparse work-level observations into temporally ordered group-level\n\fModeling Stylistic Co-evolution in Symbolic Music Heritage Collections                                                 5\n\n\n\n\n                                 Fig. 2. Distribution of musical works by country over time (1654–1940).\n\n\n\nstates, and opinion-dynamics models provide an interpretable summary of self-persistence, cross-group dependence,\nand anchoring in these long-term trajectories.\n\n3     Dataset\n3.1    Cross-Era Dataset\nWe conduct our historical analysis on the Cross-Era dataset [21, 34–38], a collection of symbolic music annotations\ncovering multiple historical periods. The dataset provides chord sequences aligned with musical works, along with\nmetadata such as composer identity and nationality.\n    The Cross-Era dataset comprises 2,000 musical works by 70 composers. Each work is represented as a sequence of\nchord labels obtained via automated chord recognition at regular temporal intervals. These chord sequences form the\nbasis for constructing harmonic representations and country-level musical trajectories in the subsequent analysis.\n    While composer-level metadata is readily available, work-level creation years are often incomplete, inconsistent, or\nabsent from the original metadata. To enable fine-grained temporal analysis, we construct an auxiliary set of work-level\nyear annotations by collecting and cross-validating information from multiple source-backed musicological resources.\nThese sources include IMSLP work pages and catalogues, Wikidata item properties, Wikipedia work pages and composer\nwork lists, RISM catalogue records, orchestra and concert-program notes, publisher catalogues, and specialized music\nreference databases.\n    For each matched work, we record the inferred composition year or year range, the supporting sources, a confidence\nlabel, and notes describing ambiguous cases, source conflicts, or cases where only a publication, premiere, or manuscript\ndate is available. The resulting annotations are released as a supplementary dataset on Hugging Face1 . This source-\nbacked annotation process provides work-level temporal information for individual compositions, allowing us to\nconduct temporal analysis at the level of individual works rather than relying solely on aggregated composer-level\n\n1 Available at https://huggingface.co/datasets/StravynDynamics/cross-era-classical-work-years\n\f6                                                                                                               He et al.\n\n\ntimelines. Figure 2 illustrates the distribution of works across 13 countries over the extended temporal range. The\nstacked bar chart shows the number of works per 5-year bin, while the line plot highlights temporal trends for individual\ncountries.\n    Based on this distribution, we restrict the subsequent opinion dynamics analysis to the period 1875–1940. This\nchoice is motivated by both data coverage and music-historical considerations. First, our opinion dynamics model\nrequires each agent to be sufficiently represented across temporal windows, so that the inferred trajectories are not\ndominated by sparse or missing observations. We therefore select the four most consistently represented national\ntraditions—Germany, Austria, France, and Russia—as separate agents, while grouping the remaining countries into a\ncomposite Others agent.\n    Second, the period 1875–1940 corresponds to a major transitional phase from late Romanticism to musical modernism.\nThe years 1875–1900 are characterized by late Romantic idioms and the flourishing of national schools, represented\nby composers such as Brahms, Tchaikovsky, Dvořák, and the early Mahler. The period 1900–1918 marks a phase of\nmodernist breakthroughs, including Impressionism, Expressionism, and the emergence of atonality, with figures such as\nDebussy, Ravel, Schoenberg, and Stravinsky reshaping harmonic and formal language. The interwar period, 1918–1940,\nfurther exhibits a pluralistic modernist landscape in which neoclassicism, twelve-tone technique, nationalist modernism,\nand jazz-inflected styles coexisted.\n    This time window is therefore particularly suitable for opinion dynamics analysis: it captures a historically dense\ntransition in which multiple musical centers and aesthetic movements competed, interacted, and influenced one another.\nGermany and Austria trace a trajectory from late Romanticism toward Expressionism, atonality, and twelve-tone\norganization; France moves from Impressionism toward neoclassicism; Russia develops from nationalist traditions into\nmodernist and émigré composer networks; and the Others agent captures the heterogeneous contributions of Czech,\nHungarian, Italian, Nordic, American, and other national or modernist styles.\n\n3.2   External Chord Corpora for Representation Learning\nThe Cross-Era dataset is used as the target corpus for the historical and cross-national analyses in this study. To avoid\nfitting the harmonic representation directly to the target corpus, we train and select the representation model using\nexternal chord corpora only. This design allows the harmonic representations assigned to works in Cross-Era to be\nobtained in a zero-shot, out-of-domain setting rather than being optimized on the same data used for the downstream\nanalysis.\n    We construct and evaluate a family of masked-language models for chord sequences, including a DeBERTa-based\nvariant that we refer to as ChordBERT2 . The goal of this stage is not to tune the representation on the downstream\nCross-Era corpus, but to select a work-level harmonic representation through experiments on external chord datasets.\n    We use three external resources with distinct roles. First, Chordonomicon [18] is used as a large source-domain\ncorpus for general chord representation learning. Although it consists primarily of contemporary and popular-music\nchord progressions, its large scale provides broad coverage of chord vocabulary and common progression patterns.\nSecond, the When-in-Rome functional-harmony corpus [10] is used as a classical-music domain-adaptation corpus.\nBecause it contains symbolic functional-harmony annotations from the classical repertoire, it allows us to test whether\ncontinued masked-language pre-training on classical harmonic material improves the learned representations. Third,\nthe DCML corpora are reserved exclusively for validation and model selection. They provide a clean, expert-annotated\n\n2 Model available at https://huggingface.co/StravynDynamics/ChordBert\n\fModeling Stylistic Co-evolution in Symbolic Music Heritage Collections                                                      7\n\nTable 1. Corpus statistics before and after filtering. Counts refer to progressions for Chordonomicon, works for When-in-Rome,\nscores for DCML, and tracks for Cross-Era.\n\n                        Corpus              Role                    Before Items     Retained Items\n                        Chordonomicon       Source pre-training          679807             679807\n                        When-in-Rome        DAPT training                   1500                 704\n                        DCML                Validation                      1283                 367\n                        Cross-Era           Target analysis                 2000               2000\n\n\n\nbenchmark of classical harmonic analyses, enabling us to evaluate whether representations trained on different corpus\ncombinations preserve musically meaningful harmonic structure.\n    Accordingly, we compare model variants trained under different data regimes, including source-domain training on\nChordonomicon, classical-domain training on When-in-Rome, and combined source-plus-domain training. All variants\nare evaluated on the held-out DCML validation set, while Cross-Era is kept entirely separate from representation\nlearning. This design ensures that the chord embedding model used for the downstream historical analysis is selected\nwithout using the target corpus for pre-training, domain adaptation, validation, model selection, or hyperparameter\ntuning.\n    To prevent leakage among the classical symbolic corpora, we construct mutually exclusive partitions with the\nfollowing priority order: Cross-Era > DCML > When-in-Rome. The Cross-Era dataset is held out entirely and all\n2,000 works are retained for the final downstream analysis. The DCML corpora are used only for validation. Works\noverlapping with Cross-Era are removed from DCML using normalized composer names, catalogue identifiers such as\nBWV, K., Op., and WoO numbers, and fuzzy title matching. Ambiguous Mozart piano sonatas without reliable Köchel\nnumbers are conservatively removed. This procedure yields a validation set of 367 works.\n    The classical domain-adaptation partition is constructed from the When-in-Rome functional-harmony corpus. Works\noverlapping with Cross-Era are removed. In addition, all composers represented in the final DCML validation set\nare held out from this training partition, preventing composer-level leakage between domain-adaptive training and\nvalidation. The resulting When-in-Rome training set contains 704 works.\n    Table 1 summarizes the corpus partitioning used in the representation-learning pipeline. Chordonomicon provides\nthe large-scale contemporary/popular-music source domain, When-in-Rome provides the classical domain-adaptation\npartition, DCML is reserved for classical validation and model selection, and Cross-Era is retained in full as the held-out\ntarget corpus for the downstream historical analysis.\n\n\n4     Chord Representation Learning\n4.1    Chord Vocabulary Normalization\nAll corpora are converted to the chord notation expected by the original ChordBERT tokenizer, a word-level whitespace\ntokenizer in which each chord and each special label (e.g., <chorus_3>) are treated as a single token, resulting in a\nvocabulary of 3,230 tokens (obtained after training on the Chordonomicon dataset’s note sequences). Representative\nconversions include C_maj → C, F#_min → Fsmin, A_maj_min7 → A7, and C_min_min7 → Cmin7.\n    Some source chord labels are not covered by the tokenizer vocabulary. We rewrite these labels into supported\nchord-token forms using fixed conversion rules, while preserving the original labels in the metadata. No-chord events\n(conventionally denoted N [12]) are retained in the raw records but are excluded during conversion, as they have no\n\f8                                                                                                                     He et al.\n\n\ncorresponding token in the ChordBERT vocabulary. We verified that all exported sequences consist exclusively of\nvalid vocabulary tokens.\n\n\n4.2    Chord Representation Models\n4.2.1 Word2Vec-style Skip-gram Chord Embeddings. We additionally consider a Word2Vec-style Skip-gram model\nfollowing the Chord2Vec approach as an embedding baseline. Let a chord sequence be denoted as (𝑐 1, 𝑐 2, . . . , 𝑐 𝐿 ), where\n                                                                                           pc\n𝐿 denotes the sequence length and ℓ indexes the position of a centre chord. Let 𝑥𝑟 (𝑐) ∈ {0, 1} indicate whether pitch\nclass 𝑟 ∈ {1, . . . , 𝑅} is present in chord 𝑐, where 𝑅 denotes the number of pitch classes.\n     Given a centre chord 𝑐 ℓ and a context chord 𝑐 ℓ+Δ , the probability of pitch class 𝑟 occurring in the context chord is\nmodelled as\n\n\n                                                       𝑝𝑟 (𝑐 ℓ ) = 𝜎 a𝑟⊤ e(𝑐 ℓ ) + 𝑏𝑟 ,\n                                                                                     \u0001\n\n     where e(𝑐 ℓ ) denotes the learned embedding of the centre chord, a𝑟 and 𝑏𝑟 are decoder parameters associated with\npitch class 𝑟 , and 𝜎 (·) is the logistic sigmoid function.\n     Assuming that pitch classes are conditionally independent given the centre chord, the Skip-gram objective is defined\nas\n\n                                 𝐿\n                                ∑︁     𝑅\n                                   ∑︁ ∑︁ \u0002 pc                               pc                            \u0003\n                      LSG = −             𝑥𝑟 (𝑐 ℓ+Δ ) log 𝑝𝑟 (𝑐 ℓ ) + (1 − 𝑥𝑟 (𝑐 ℓ+Δ )) log(1 − 𝑝𝑟 (𝑐 ℓ )) .                 (1)\n                                ℓ=1 Δ∈ C (ℓ ) 𝑟 =1\n     where\n\n\n                                              C(ℓ) = {Δ : 1 ≤ ℓ + Δ ≤ 𝐿, 0 < |Δ| ≤ 𝑢}\n     denotes the set of valid context offsets within a context window of radius 𝑢.\n     After training, a fixed-size representation of a musical work 𝑚 is obtained by averaging the learned chord embeddings:\n\n                                                                      𝐿\n                                                                      𝑚\n                                                          SG       1 ∑︁\n                                                         v𝑚  =           e(𝑐𝑚,ℓ ),                                           (2)\n                                                                  𝐿𝑚 ℓ=1\n     where 𝐿𝑚 denotes the number of chords in work 𝑚 and 𝑐𝑚,ℓ denotes the ℓ-th chord of that work.\n\n4.2.2 Transformer-based model. We evaluate two Transformer-based masked-language models for chord representation\nlearning: a RoBERTa encoder [20] and a DeBERTa encoder [13]. Both models use the same ChordBERT tokenizer and\nvocabulary, so differences in downstream performance can be attributed to the encoder architecture and training regime\nrather than to differences in chord normalization. The initial ChordBERT checkpoint is trained on Chordonomicon,\nwhich contains 679,807 contemporary chord progressions. We use 543,845 progressions for training, 67,980 for validation,\nand 67,982 for testing.\n     The models are trained with a masked language modelling (MLM) objective [5]. Given a chord-token sequence\nc = (𝑐 1, . . . , 𝑐 𝐿 ), and a set of masked positions M, let c̃ denote the corrupted input sequence after masking the positions\nin M. The MLM objective is\n\n                                                                 1 ∑︁\n                                                     LMLM = −         log 𝑝𝜃 (𝑐 ℓ | c̃).                                     (3)\n                                                                |M|\n                                                                      ℓ∈M\n\fModeling Stylistic Co-evolution in Symbolic Music Heritage Collections                                                             9\n\nTable 2. Chord- and work-level evaluation on the held-out DCML validation set. Lower is better for MLM loss; higher is better for all\nother metrics. “C” denotes training on Chordonomicon, “W” denotes training on the leakage-free When-in-Rome training partition,\nand “C&W” denotes training on both datasets. Dashes indicate inapplicable metrics: Chord2Vec predicts pitch-class context rather\nthan masked discrete chord tokens and therefore does not support chord-level MLM evaluation.\n\n\n                          Chord2Vec baseline                              Transformer-based model\nLevel Metric                 Skip-gram                                RoBERTa                DeBERTa\n                      Base        C       W      C&W Base             C        W      C&W Base              C        W      C&W\n      MLM                –        –        –        –     8.1683 2.5042 6.5674 2.3951 8.1270 2.0286 6.5660 1.7906\n      Top-1              –        –        –        –     0.0003 0.3476 0.0555 0.3533 0.0000 0.5326 0.1085 0.5472\nChord\n      Top-10             –        –        –        –     0.0044 0.8367 0.3690 0.8418 0.0003 0.8492 0.4485 0.8876\n      MRR                –        –        –        –     0.0030 0.5075 0.1552 0.5173 0.0014 0.6391 0.2051 0.6645\n     R@1    0.1825 0.1865 0.2024 0.1865 0.2698 0.2619 0.2540 0.2738 0.2302 0.3056 0.2381 0.2817\nSame R@10   0.7540 0.7341 0.7381 0.7659 0.8016 0.7976 0.8016 0.8056 0.7937 0.8294 0.8056 0.8175\nwork MRR    0.3619 0.3605 0.3606 0.3624 0.4303 0.4340 0.4221 0.4441 0.4089 0.4674 0.4128 0.4548\n     mAP@10 0.2049 0.1959 0.1875 0.2009 0.2582 0.2483 0.2559 0.2503 0.2544 0.2843 0.2494 0.2790\n\nSplit     R@1         0.3270 0.2616 0.2779 0.2970 0.5204 0.5368 0.5123 0.5395 0.4278 0.4959 0.4114 0.4768\n          R@10        0.7275 0.6975 0.6812 0.7193 0.8692 0.9237 0.8692 0.9292 0.8011 0.8556 0.7956 0.8365\nhalf      MRR         0.4541 0.3956 0.4096 0.4211 0.6295 0.6647 0.6215 0.6686 0.5499 0.6082 0.5372 0.5940\n\n\n   This objective encourages the encoder to infer masked chord tokens from their surrounding harmonic context.\n   In addition, we apply an unsupervised SimCSE-style contrastive objective [9]. For each chord sequence in a batch,\ntwo dropout-augmented representations are generated, denoted as h𝑏 and h𝑏+ . The contrastive objective is\n\n                                                      𝐵\n                                                   1 ∑︁        exp(sim(h𝑏 , h𝑏+ )/𝜏𝑐 )\n                                         LCL = −         log Í𝐵                  +\n                                                                                         ,                                       (4)\n                                                              𝑎=1 exp(sim(h𝑏 , h𝑎 )/𝜏𝑐 )\n                                                   𝐵\n                                                     𝑏=1\n   where 𝐵 denotes the batch size, sim(·, ·) is cosine similarity, and 𝜏𝑐 is the temperature parameter.\n   The total training objective is\n\n\n                                                        L = LMLM + 𝜆LCL,                                                         (5)\n   where 𝜆 = 0.1 controls the contribution of the contrastive objective and is fixed for all experiments.\n\n4.3     Domain-Adaptive Pre-training\nWe trained for 3 epochs using AdamW with a batch size of 64, an initial learning rate of 5 × 10−4 , weight decay of\n0.01, and gradient clipping at 1.0. A cosine learning-rate schedule with a warm-up phase covering the first 5% of\noptimization steps is used. At each step, 15% of tokens are dynamically selected for masking following the standard\n80/10/10 corruption strategy: 80% are replaced by [MASK], 10% by a random chord token, and 10% are left unchanged.\n\n4.4     Evaluation Protocol and Model Selection\nWe evaluate three families of chord representation models: a non-contextual Skip-gram baseline, a RoBERTa-based\nmasked-language model, and a DeBERTa-based masked-language model that we refer to as ChordBERT. The Transformer-", "numLines": 400, "startLine": 1, "totalLines": 1273}}}
