{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-ae699d1b600fb304", "type": "tool_result", "content": "400\tmasked-language model, and a DeBERTa-based masked-language model that we refer to as ChordBERT. The Transformer-\n401\tbased models are evaluated at both the chord level and the work level, whereas the Skip-gram baseline is evaluated\n402\t\f10                                                                                                              He et al.\n403\t\n404\t\n405\tonly at the work level because it does not define a masked-token prediction distribution over the full chord vocabulary.\n406\tFor chord-level evaluation, we apply dynamic masking to the held-out DCML validation set. The evaluation comprises\n407\t11,552 masked chord-token instances and is conducted for the RoBERTa- and DeBERTa-based models. We report\n408\tmasked-language-modeling (MLM) loss, Top-1 accuracy, Top-10 accuracy, and mean reciprocal rank (MRR). Because\n409\teach masked position has a single ground-truth token, mean average precision is not reported at the chord level, as it is\n410\tequivalent to MRR in this setting. For work-level evaluation, each model is converted into a fixed-size representation.\n411\tFor the Transformer-based models, token-level hidden states are mean-pooled within each input chunk after excluding\n412\tspecial and padding tokens. The resulting chunk representations are then aggregated using the number of valid chord\n413\ttokens in each chunk as weights. For the Skip-gram baseline, the work-level representation is obtained by averaging\n414\tthe learned chord embeddings over the complete chord sequence. We evaluate these representations using same-work\n415\tmovement retrieval and split-half identity retrieval. For both protocols, we report Recall@1, Recall@10, and MRR. We\n416\tadditionally report mAP@10 for same-work movement retrieval, where a query may have multiple relevant movements\n417\tfrom the same composition. Formal definitions of all chord-level and work-level evaluation metrics are provided in\n418\tAppendix A.\n419\t     To quantify the effect of pre-training, we also compare against randomly initialized Transformer baselines with the\n420\tsame tokenizer, architecture, pooling operation, and validation data as their corresponding trained models. Table 2\n421\treports the chord- and work-level evaluation results. Continued masked-language pre-training on the When-in-Rome\n422\tcorpus improves chord-level prediction for the Transformer models. For the DeBERTa-based ChordBERT model, adding\n423\tWhen-in-Rome training after Chordonomicon reduces MLM loss from 2.0286 to 1.7906, increases Top-1 accuracy\n424\tfrom 0.5326 to 0.5472, increases Top-10 accuracy from 0.8492 to 0.8876, and improves MRR from 0.6391 to 0.6645. A\n425\tsimilar pattern is observed for the RoBERTa-based model, indicating that classical-domain adaptation improves local\n426\tmasked-chord prediction.\n427\t     The work-level retrieval results show a different pattern. The DeBERTa-based ChordBERT model trained on Chor-\n428\tdonomicon achieves the strongest same-work movement retrieval performance among the evaluated representations,\n429\twith Recall@1 of 0.3056, Recall@10 of 0.8294, MRR of 0.4674, and mAP@10 of 0.2843. These metrics are most directly\n430\taligned with our downstream use case, where each musical work must be represented by a stable work-level harmonic\n431\tembedding. Although continued When-in-Rome pre-training improves chord-level prediction, it slightly lowers the\n432\tDeBERTa work-level retrieval scores. This suggests that the masked-language objective improves local harmonic\n433\tprediction but does not necessarily optimize the mean-pooled work-level representation used in the historical dynamics\n434\tanalysis.\n435\t     For the downstream analysis, we therefore select the DeBERTa-based ChordBERT variant trained on Chordo-\n436\tnomicon as the main harmonic embedding model. The RoBERTa-based models provide competitive split-half retrieval\n437\tperformance, and the Skip-gram model provides a non-contextual baseline, but the Chordonomicon-trained Chord-\n438\tBERT model offers the best same-work organization of the embedding space. We use its work-level embeddings as the\n439\tharmonic representation layer for the subsequent opinion-dynamics analysis.\n440\t\n441\t\n442\t5    Opinion-Dynamics Analysis\n443\tBased on the temporal distribution of annotated works (Fig. 2), we retain Russia, France, Germany and Austria as\n444\tseparate cultural groups because they provide the most continuous coverage across the 1875–1940 analysis window.\n445\tWorks from the remaining represented countries—Norway, Finland, Czechia, Italy, Hungary, and the United States—are\n446\t\fModeling Stylistic Co-evolution in Symbolic Music Heritage Collections                                               11\n447\t\n448\t\n449\tpooled into an “Others” category to avoid discarding sparsely represented material. This category is used only as a\n450\tpooled comparison group and is not interpreted as a coherent national or cultural tradition.\n451\t  Annual observations are sparse for several groups. We therefore sample the reconstructed trajectories at five-year\n452\tintervals, which provides a compromise between temporal resolution and the number of observed works available\n453\tat each time point. The resulting analysis grid spans 1875–1940. We use the states from 1875–1925 for parameter\n454\testimation and reserve the final three states, corresponding to 1930, 1935, and 1940, as a chronological holdout set for\n455\trecursive retrospective evaluation.\n456\t\n457\t5.1   Country-level opinion state construction\n458\tWe construct country-level opinion states from work-level harmonic representations. In this paper, an “opinion” does\n459\tnot denote a subjective attitude, but a multidimensional harmonic-style state derived from symbolic chord sequences.\n460\tLet 𝐾 denote the number of cultural groups and let 𝐷 denote the number of retained PCA dimensions.\n461\t  The construction proceeds in four steps. First, each work is encoded as a contextual chord embedding. Second, work\n462\tembeddings are projected into a low-dimensional PCA space. Third, each PCA coordinate is normalized to a common\n463\tscale. Fourth, annual country-level observations are smoothed through a causal Kalman filter. The resulting filtered\n464\tstates form the matrix X(𝑡) used by the DG and FJ models.\n465\t\n466\t5.1.1 Step 1: Work embedding with ChordBERT. Each musical work is represented by a 256-dimensional contextual\n467\tembedding obtained from ChordBERT, a DeBERTa-based masked-language model for chord sequences. The chord\n468\tsequence of work 𝑤 is tokenized and, because the model has a maximum input length of 256 tokens, truncated to at\n469\tmost 254 chord tokens, leaving space for the tokenizer-added boundary tokens.\n470\t  Let 𝐿𝑤 denote the tokenized length of work 𝑤 and let 𝑚 𝑤ℓ ∈ {0, 1} be the attention-mask value at token position ℓ.\n471\tThe work-level embedding is obtained by masked mean pooling over non-padding positions:\n472\t                                                  Í𝐿𝑤\n473\t                                                       𝑚 𝑤ℓ h𝑤ℓ\n474\t                                             v𝑤 = ℓ=1\n475\t                                                    Í𝐿𝑤         ,                                                    (6)\n476\t                                                      ℓ=1 𝑚 𝑤ℓ\n477\t\n478\twhere h𝑤ℓ ∈ R256 is the last-layer hidden state of the DeBERTa encoder at position ℓ and padding positions are excluded\n479\tby the attention mask. This produces one fixed-size contextual harmonic embedding v𝑤 ∈ R256 for each work.\n480\t\n481\t5.1.2 Step 2: PCA dimensionality reduction. Principal component analysis is fitted on the ChordBERT embeddings of\n482\tworks dated between 1875 and 1940. Let\n483\t\n484\t                                         W1875:1940 = {𝑤 : 1875 ≤ 𝜏𝑤 ≤ 1940}\n485\t\n486\tdenote this PCA fitting sample, where 𝜏𝑤 is the annotated composition year of work 𝑤. Let Vfit ∈ R480×256 be the\n487\tcorresponding matrix of work-level embeddings. PCA maps each work embedding to an orthogonal coordinate vector:\n488\t\n489\t                                       z𝑤 = (𝑧 𝑤1, 𝑧 𝑤2, . . . , 𝑧 𝑤𝐷 ) = (v𝑤 − v̄)A𝐷 ,                              (7)\n490\t\n491\twhere v̄ is the mean embedding in the PCA fitting sample and A𝐷 ∈ R256×𝐷 contains the first 𝐷 principal directions.\n492\t  We retain the first 𝐷 = 23 components. Let 𝜌𝑑 denote the explained-variance ratio of component 𝑑. The retained\n493\t                   Í𝐷\n494\tcomponents satisfy 𝑑=1  𝜌𝑑 = 0.903726 and therefore explain approximately 90.37% of the total embedding variance, as\n495\tshown in Fig. 3. The PCA transformation fitted on the 1875–1940 sample is also applied to works preceding 1875. These\n496\tearlier observations are used only to initialize the subsequent forward Kalman-filter recursion.\n497\t\f12                                                                                                                               He et al.\n498\t\n499\t\n500\t\n501\t\n502\t                                    Fig. 3. Explained variance of the retained PCA components.\n503\t\n504\t\n505\t\n506\t5.1.3 Step 3: Component-wise min–max normalization. After PCA projection, each retained component is normalized\n507\tindependently. For component 𝑑, the minimum and maximum are computed over the PCA fitting sample:\n508\t\n509\t                                      𝑧𝑑min =       min          𝑧 𝑤𝑑 ,        𝑧𝑑max =          max          𝑧 𝑤𝑑 .                    (8)\n510\t                                                𝑤 ∈ W1875:1940                              𝑤 ∈ W1875:1940\n511\t\n512\t     The normalized score of work 𝑤 on component 𝑑 is\n513\t                                                         𝑧 𝑤𝑑 − 𝑧𝑑min\n514\t                                                𝑧˜𝑤𝑑 =                    ,         𝑑 = 1, . . . , 𝐷.                                  (9)\n515\t                                                         𝑧𝑑max − 𝑧𝑑min\n516\t     For works in the 1875–1940 fitting sample, each retained component has minimum zero and maximum one. When\n517\tthe same transformation is applied to works outside this period, the resulting values are clipped to the unit interval:\n518\t\n519\t                                                    𝑢 𝑤𝑑 = min {1, max {0, 𝑧˜𝑤𝑑 }} .                                                  (10)\n520\t\n521\tThe value 𝑢 𝑤𝑑 is the final normalized work-level score used in the country-level aggregation. Normalization is performed\n522\tseparately for each PCA dimension and is not computed jointly across components or cultural groups.\n523\t\n524\t5.1.4 Step 4: Causal Kalman filtering. For cultural group 𝑖, PCA dimension 𝑑, and calendar year 𝑡, let W𝑖 (𝑡) denote the\n525\tset of works assigned to group 𝑖 with annotated composition year 𝑡. Country assignments account for a composer’s\n526\tmigration history when such information is available. If 𝑁𝑖 (𝑡) = |W𝑖 (𝑡)| > 0, the annual observation is\n527\t\n528\t                                                                      1          ∑︁\n529\t                                                     𝑦𝑖𝑑 (𝑡) =                              𝑢 𝑤𝑑 ,                                    (11)\n530\t                                                                    𝑁𝑖 (𝑡)\n531\t                                                                              𝑤 ∈ W𝑖 (𝑡 )\n532\t     where 𝑢 𝑤𝑑 is the normalized score of work 𝑤 on PCA dimension 𝑑. If 𝑁𝑖 (𝑡) = 0, the observation is treated as missing.\n533\t     We model 𝑦𝑖𝑑 (𝑡) as a noisy observation of a latent country-level harmonic state 𝑠𝑖𝑑 (𝑡). For each group–dimension\n534\tpair, the state follows a random-walk model:\n535\t\n536\t\n537\t                          𝑠𝑖𝑑 (𝑡) = 𝑠𝑖𝑑 (𝑡 − 1) + 𝜂𝑖𝑑 (𝑡),                                           𝜂𝑖𝑑 (𝑡) ∼ N (0, 𝑄),              (12)\n538\t                         𝑦𝑖𝑑 (𝑡) = 𝑠𝑖𝑑 (𝑡) + 𝜖𝑖𝑑 (𝑡),                                                𝜖𝑖𝑑 (𝑡) ∼ N (0, 𝑅𝑖 (𝑡)) .        (13)\n539\t\n540\t     The process variance is fixed at 𝑄 = 0.0005 for all cultural groups and PCA dimensions. Because each annual\n541\tobservation is an average over 𝑁𝑖 (𝑡) works, we use the inverse-sample-size approximation\n542\t\fModeling Stylistic Co-evolution in Symbolic Music Heritage Collections                                                                  13\n543\t\n544\t\n545\t\n546\t\n547\t                                           Fig. 4. Country-level opinion trajectories on PC1\n548\t\n549\t\n550\t\n551\t\n552\t                                                 𝑅0\n553\t                                                    𝑅𝑖 (𝑡) =\n554\t                                                      ,    𝑅0 = 0.01.                                       (14)\n555\t                                               𝑁𝑖 (𝑡)\n556\t   Thus, annual observations based on more works are treated as more reliable and receive greater weight in the\n557\tfiltering update. This specification is a parsimonious approximation to the variance of a sample mean; the empirical\n558\twithin-year variance is recorded for diagnostic purposes but is not used in the measurement model.\n559\t   For each group–dimension pair, the filter is initialized at the earliest available observation year 𝑡 0,𝑖𝑑 : 𝑠ˆ𝑖𝑑 (𝑡 0,𝑖𝑑 |\n560\t𝑡 0,𝑖𝑑 ) = 𝑦𝑖𝑑 (𝑡 0,𝑖𝑑 ), 𝑃𝑖𝑑 (𝑡 0,𝑖𝑑 | 𝑡 0,𝑖𝑑 ) = 𝑅𝑖 (𝑡 0,𝑖𝑑 ). The recursion is then run strictly forward in time. When an observation is\n561\tavailable, the state is updated according to its uncertainty 𝑅𝑖 (𝑡); when it is missing, only the prediction step is applied.\n562\tConsequently, 𝑠ˆ𝑖𝑑 (𝑡 | 𝑡) depends only on observations available at or before year 𝑡.\n563\t   For the subsequent dynamics analysis, the filtered annual trajectories are sampled at five-year intervals. Let T =\n564\t{1875, 1880, . . . , 1940} denote the analysis grid. For each 𝑡 ∈ T , define 𝑥𝑖𝑑 (𝑡) = 𝑠ˆ𝑖𝑑 (𝑡 | 𝑡) and collect the filtered states\n565\tinto X(𝑡) = [𝑥𝑖𝑑 (𝑡)] 𝑖=1,...,𝐾; 𝑑=1,...,𝐷 ∈ R𝐾 ×𝐷 . The matrix X(𝑡) is the multidimensional harmonic-state representation\n566\tused as input to the DG and FJ models. PCA projection and component-wise scaling are estimated over the full 1875–\n567\t1940 analysis window to define a shared retrospective coordinate system, whereas the dynamics models are fitted on\n568\t1875–1925 and evaluated recursively on 1930–1940.\n569\t   For visualization, Fig. 4 shows the filtered country-level trajectories on PC1. Trajectories for all retained PCA\n570\tdimensions are reported in Appendix B.1, Fig. 6.\n571\t\n572\t\n573\t5.2   Opinion Dynamics Modeling\n574\t5.2.1 Multi-dimensional DG model. Let X(𝑡) ∈ R𝐾 ×𝐷 collect the five countries’ Kalman-filtered harmonic opinion\n575\tvectors at time 𝑡, with 𝐷 ≤ 23. Rows correspond to country groups and columns correspond to retained PCA coordinates.\n576\tThus, each country is represented by a 𝐷-dimensional harmonic state vector.\n577\t   A general bilinear multidimensional transition could be written as\n578\t\n579\t                                                         X(𝑡 + 1) = CWX(𝑡),                                                           (15)\n580\t\n581\twhere W ∈ R𝐾 ×𝐾 models cross-country dependence and C ∈ R𝐷 ×𝐷 models cross-component dependence among PCA\n582\tcoordinates. In the main analysis, we use a restricted multidimensional DG specification by setting C = I𝐷 :\n583\t\n584\t                                         X(𝑡 + 1) = WX(𝑡),            W1 = 1,      0 ≤ 𝑊𝑖 𝑗 ≤ 1.                                      (16)\n585\t\f14                                                                                                                 He et al.\n586\t\n587\t\n588\t     Equivalently, for country 𝑖,\n589\t                                                               ∑︁\n590\t                                                X𝑖 (𝑡 + 1) =            𝑊𝑖 𝑗 X 𝑗 (𝑡),                                   (17)\n591\t                                                                   𝑗\n592\t\n593\twhere X𝑖 (𝑡) ∈ R𝐷 is the harmonic state vector of country 𝑖. This formulation is multidimensional in the sense that\n594\tthe opinion state of each country is vector-valued, but the same country-level influence matrix W is shared across all\n595\tretained PCA coordinates and no lagged cross-component transition is estimated.\n596\t     Under this convention, rows of W correspond to target countries and columns correspond to source countries.\n597\tTherefore, 𝑊𝑖 𝑗 measures the contribution of source country 𝑗 to the next-period harmonic state of target country 𝑖.\n598\tThe restriction C = I𝐷 is a parsimonious modeling choice. It does not imply that orthogonality alone rules out every\n599\tpossible lagged cross-component effect; rather, it allows the same country-level influence matrix to be estimated across\n600\tall retained PCA dimensions. For later use, define the DG residual as\n601\t\n602\t                                             RDG (𝑡; W) = X(𝑡 + 1) − WX(𝑡).                                             (18)\n603\t\n604\t5.2.2 FJ model. The multidimensional FJ (FJ) specification augments social influence with anchoring to each country’s\n605\tintrinsic harmonic opinion matrix Z ∈ R𝐾 ×𝐷 :\n606\t\n607\t                                           X(𝑡 + 1) = SCWX(𝑡) + (I𝐾 − S)Z,                                              (19)\n608\t\n609\twhere C = I𝐷 , Z = X(1875), S = diag(𝑠 1, . . . , 𝑠𝐾 ), and 0 ≤ 𝑠𝑖 ≤ 1. Under this parameterization, 𝑠𝑖 is susceptibility to\n610\tsocial influence: 𝑠𝑖 = 1 gives the DG limit, whereas 𝑠𝑖 = 0 corresponds to complete attachment to the intrinsic state.\n611\tHence, cultural stubbornness is measured by 1 − 𝑠𝑖 , not by 𝑠𝑖 .\n612\t     For stable estimation, we reparameterize the model in terms of the effective influence matrix\n613\t                                                                  ∑︁\n614\t                                      A = SW,       𝐴𝑖 𝑗 ≥ 0,         𝐴𝑖 𝑗 = 𝑠𝑖 ≤ 1.                                    (20)\n615\t                                                                             𝑗\n616\t\n617\tThus, A is a substochastic matrix whose row sum gives the susceptibility of the corresponding country. This reparame-\n618\tterization converts the bilinear parameterization in (S, W) into a constrained least-squares problem over A. Equivalently,\n619\tfor target country 𝑖, the FJ transition can be written as\n620\t                                                              ∑︁            \u0002           \u0003\n621\t                                          X𝑖 (𝑡 + 1) = Z𝑖 +            𝐴𝑖 𝑗 X 𝑗 (𝑡) − Z𝑖 ,                              (21)\n622\t                                                               𝑗\n623\t                                                                          Í\n624\twhich is linear in the row A𝑖 under the constraints 𝐴𝑖 𝑗 ≥ 0 and              𝑗 𝐴𝑖 𝑗 ≤ 1.\n625\t     The FJ residual is\n626\t                                  RFJ (𝑡; A) = X(𝑡 + 1) − AX(𝑡) − [I − diag(A1)] Z.                                    (22)\n627\t                                   Í                                          𝐴\n628\t                                                                              b𝑖 𝑗\n629\tAfter estimating A,\n630\t                 b we recover b\n631\t                              𝑠𝑖 = 𝑗 𝐴  b𝑖 𝑗 and, for rows with b\n632\t                                                                𝑠𝑖 > 0,𝑊\n633\t                                                                       b𝑖 𝑗 =\n634\t                                                                              𝑠𝑖\n635\t                                                                              b\n636\t                                                                                   . Thus, W contains the row-stochastic FJ\n637\tinfluence weights, whereas A = SW is the effective social influence operator used in the transition equation.\n638\t\n639\t5.2.3 Equal-weight and PCA-variance-weighted objective functions. Both the DG and FJ models are estimated on the\n640\tsame five-country state matrices and the same 1875–1925 training period. The years 1930, 1935, and 1940 are held out\n641\tand predicted recursively as one-, two-, and three-step-ahead forecasts, respectively.\n642\t     Let Ttrain denote the set of training transitions, and let 𝑚 ∈ {DG, FJ} index the dynamical model. Each model is\n643\tspecified by a parameter matrix 𝜽 𝑚 , a residual function R𝑚 (𝑡; 𝜽 𝑚 ), and a feasible parameter set Θ𝑚 .\n644\t     For the DG model, 𝜽 DG = W with feasible set ΘDG = {W : W ≥ 0, W1 = 1}, And for the FJ model, we estimate the\n645\teffective influence matrix 𝜽 FJ = A = SW with feasible set ΘFJ = {A : A ≥ 0, A1 ≤ 1} .\n646\t\fModeling Stylistic Co-evolution in Symbolic Music Heritage Collections                                                       15\n647\t\n648\t\n649\t  To define equal-weight and PCA-variance-weighted estimation in a unified form, let 𝑞 ∈ {EW, VW} denote the\n650\testimation scheme. For the equal-weight scheme, all retained PCA coordinates receive the same weight: G𝐷(EW) = I𝐷 .\n651\t  For the PCA-variance-weighted scheme, let 𝑣𝑑 denote the explained-variance ratio of PC𝑑, and define\n652\t                                            𝐷\n653\t                          𝑣𝑑               ∑︁\n654\t                   𝜔𝑑 = Í𝐷            ,           𝜔𝑑 = 1,          𝛀𝐷 = diag(𝜔 1, . . . , 𝜔 𝐷 ),          G𝐷(VW) = 𝛀𝐷\n655\t                                                                                                                    1/2\n656\t                                                                                                                        .   (23)\n657\t                            𝑘=1 𝑣 𝑘        𝑑=1\n658\t\n659\t  The objective function for model 𝑚 under estimation scheme 𝑞 is\n660\t                                                                      (𝑞) 2\n661\t                                       (𝑞)\n662\t                                                   ∑︁\n663\t                                      L𝑚 (𝜽 𝑚 ) =        R𝑚 (𝑡; 𝜽 𝑚 )G𝐷     .                                               (24)\n664\t                                                                                                 𝐹\n665\t                                                               𝑡 ∈ Ttrain\n666\t\n667\tThe corresponding parameter estimate is obtained by solving the constrained optimization problem\n668\t                                            (𝑞)          (𝑞)\n669\t                                          𝜽 𝑚 = arg min L𝑚 (𝜽 𝑚 )\n670\t                                          b                                         s.t. 𝜽 𝑚 ∈ Θ𝑚 .                         (25)\n671\t                                                        𝜽𝑚\n672\t\n673\t  Equivalently, if r𝑚,𝑑 (𝑡; 𝜽 𝑚 ) denotes the 𝑑-th column of the residual matrix R𝑚 (𝑡; 𝜽 𝑚 ), then\n674\t                                                                  𝐷\n675\t                                                                                                     2\n676\t                                            (𝑞)\n677\t                                                              ∑︁ ∑︁           (𝑞)\n678\t                                          L𝑚 (𝜽 𝑚 ) =                        𝛼𝑑      r𝑚,𝑑 (𝑡; 𝜽 𝑚 ) 2 ,                     (26)\n679\t                                                            𝑡 ∈ Ttrain 𝑑=1\n680\t\n681\twhere\n682\t                                                    𝛼𝑑(EW) = 1,              𝛼𝑑(VW) = 𝜔𝑑 .                                  (27)\n683\tThus, the equal-weight objective assigns the same importance to every retained PCA coordinate, whereas the variance-\n684\tweighted objective gives greater weight to coordinates explaining more embedding variance.\n685\t  The same normalized PCA weights are used for both dynamical specifications. Thus, differences between the equal-\n686\tweight and variance-weighted estimators are attributable solely to the relative importance assigned to the response\n687\tdimensions. PCA weighting modifies only the objective function, not the transition equation. The DG forecasts are\n688\t            b + 1) = WX(𝑡),\n689\tcomputed as X(𝑡       b     whereas the FJ forecasts are computed as\n690\t                                                         h             i\n691\t                                    b + 1) = AX(𝑡)\n692\t                                    X(𝑡        b       + I − diag( A1)\n693\t                                                                   b     Z.                                  (28)\n694\t\n695\tIn all cases, C = I𝐷 . Setting C = 𝛀𝐷 would instead impose component-specific attenuation at every recursive step and\n696\twould define a different dynamical model.\n697\t\n698\t\n699\t5.2.4 Evaluation design and model comparison. All models are evaluated using the same chronological split. Model\n700\tparameters are estimated from the ten five-year transitions between 1875 and 1925. The states in 1930, 1935, and 1940\n701\tare held out. Starting from the observed 1925 state, the three test states are forecast recursively: the 1930 prediction is\n702\tused as the input for 1935, and the 1935 prediction is used as the input for 1940. No observed test state is fed back into\n703\tthe model. The Kalman opinion signals are computed by forward filtering, so the filtered state at each year uses only\n704\tobservations available at that year or earlier. PCA projection and component-wise normalization are defined over the\n705\tfull 1875–1940 analysis window, providing a shared retrospective coordinate system for the evaluation.\n706\t              e𝑖𝑑 (𝑡) − 𝑋𝑖𝑑 (𝑡) denote a recursive-trajectory error, where X(𝑡)\n707\t   Let 𝑒𝑡𝑖𝑑 = 𝑋                                                            e is the model-generated state and X(𝑡)\n708\tis the filtered reference state. For the training-trajectory metrics, the model is initialized once at the observed 1875\n709\tstate and then recursively propagated through 1925 without being reset to the observations. The initial 1875 error is\n710\ttherefore zero by construction.\n711\t\f16                                                                                                                    He et al.\n712\t\n713\t\n714\t     To separate the parameter-estimation objective from the evaluation metric, each fitted model is scored under two\n715\tevaluation schemes. Let 𝑝 ∈ {EW, VW} index the evaluation scheme, and define component weights\n716\t                                                                                        𝐷\n717\t                                                  1                                    ∑︁         (𝑝 )\n718\t                                       𝛽𝑑(EW) =     ,     𝛽𝑑(VW) = 𝜔𝑑 ,                          𝛽𝑑      = 1.              (29)\n719\t                                                  𝐷\n720\t                                                                                       𝑑=1\n721\t\n722\twhere 𝑣𝑑 is the explained-variance ratio of PC𝑑. The equal-weight scheme therefore assigns the same evaluation weight\n723\tto every retained coordinate, whereas the variance-weighted scheme gives greater evaluation weight to coordinates\n724\texplaining more embedding variance.\n725\t     Let Trec denote the set of recursive training states used for trajectory evaluation, with 𝑇 = |Trec | = 11. For evaluation\n726\tscheme 𝑝, the recursive MAE is\n727\t                                                          𝐷                        𝐾\n728\t                                                         ∑︁      (𝑝 )    1 ∑︁ ∑︁\n729\t       