{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b85215cafd4a22ac", "type": "tool_result", "content": "800\t                         Panel B: PCA-variance-weighted evaluation\n801\t                                   Equal         0.0458 0.0587 0.0308        0.0444    0.0525   0.0455\n802\t                         DG\n803\t                                   PCA-weight 0.0449 0.0579 0.0298           0.0445    0.0529   0.0453\n804\t                                  Equal          0.0458   0.0585    0.0329    0.0465   0.0522   0.0454\n805\t                         FJ\n806\t                                  PCA-weight     0.0442   0.0570    0.0313    0.0450   0.0519   0.0452\n807\t\n808\t\n809\t\n810\tAppendix B.2 shows that the estimated DG influence weights become comparatively stable as the number of retained\n811\tPCA dimensions increases.\n812\t\n813\t   Results under ordinary evaluation. PCA-weighted estimation improves the recursive MAE and mean RMSE of all\n814\tthree model families, although the changes are small. The clearest improvement is for FJ: its MAE decreases from\n815\t0.03903 to 0.03854, and its mean RMSE decreases from 0.04964 to 0.04922, the best values in Panel A. PCA-weighted DG\n816\tprovides the most accurate early forecasts, with RMSE 0.02945 in 1930 and 0.04659 in 1935. At the longest horizon,\n817\thowever, equal-weight FJ is best with RMSE 0.05190. Equal-weight FJ also has the smallest one-step training residual,\n818\t0.0419. Its weighted counterpart sacrifices a small amount of one-step fit but improves the recursively accumulated\n819\ttraining errors. This difference illustrates why a one-step residual and a recursive-path metric need not rank models\n820\tidentically.\n821\t\n822\t   Results under PCA-variance-weighted evaluation. The advantage of weighted estimation becomes more visible when\n823\terrors on the leading principal components receive greater importance. PCA-weighted FJ has the lowest MAE (0.04424),\n824\tmean RMSE (0.05703), 1940 RMSE (0.05192), and weighted residual (0.10220). PCA-weighted DG remains best for the\n825\t1930 forecast, with RMSE 0.02981, whereas equal-weight DG is marginally best in 1935, with RMSE 0.04435 compared\n826\twith 0.04445 for weighted DG. Consequently, variance weighting improves the overall representation of the more\n827\tinformative PCA directions, but it does not guarantee lower error at every individual forecast horizon.\n828\t\n829\t5.2.5 Parameter interpretation. Fig. 5 shows the estimated country-level dependency matrices for the 23-dimensional\n830\tmodels with PCA variance weighted. Rows denote target countries and columns denote source countries, so an entry in\n831\trow 𝑖 and column 𝑗 represents the contribution of source country 𝑗 to the next-period harmonic state of target country\n832\t𝑖. The DG panel displays the estimated row-stochastic matrix W.b The FJ panel displays the row-stochastic matrix W     b\n833\trecovered from the effective matrix A  SW;\n834\t                                    b =b                                                                             SW.\n835\t                                         b the effective social influence operator in the FJ transition is therefore b b\n836\t   The diagonal entries of W\n837\t                           b summarize self-persistence in each cultural trajectory. In the DG model, Russia, France,\n838\tGermany, Austria, and Others have self-dependency weights of approximately 0.612, 0.629, 0.633, 0.559, and 0.649,\n839\trespectively. This indicates that each group’s next-period harmonic state is explained primarily by its own previous\n840\t\f18                                                                                                                       He et al.\n841\t\n842\t\n843\t\n844\t\n845\t                          (a) DG                                                                (b) FJ\n846\t\n847\tFig. 5. Estimated DG influence matrix and FJ row-stochastic influence weights from the 23-dimensional models. Rows denote target\n848\tcountries and columns denote source countries. The FJ panel displays the row-stochastic matrix W before susceptibility scaling; its\n849\teffective social influence operator is SW.\n850\t\n851\t\n852\t\n853\t\n854\tstate, rather than by immediate replacement through external sources. This pattern is consistent with the persistence\n855\tof national and institutional traditions during the transition from late Romanticism to early modernism: German\n856\tand Austrian harmonic practices remained connected to the Austro-German lineage of late Romantic chromaticism\n857\tand Viennese modernism, French harmonic development retained a distinct trajectory from Impressionism toward\n858\tneoclassicism, and Russian music combined national-school inheritances with modernist [2, 3, 11].\n859\t     The off-diagonal entries reveal the main cross-group dependencies estimated by the model. The DG matrix recovers\n860\ta close German–Austrian relationship: Austria assigns a substantial weight to Germany, while Germany also assigns a\n861\tnon-negligible weight to Austria. This is historically plausible given the shared Austro-German harmonic environment\n862\tlinking late Romantic idioms, Mahler’s expanded tonal language, and Viennese modernism [30]. The French and German\n863\ttrajectories also show asymmetric cross-dependence, reflecting a broader European context in which French harmonic\n864\tcolor, modal sonority, and tonal ambiguity developed in dialogue with the dominant German Romantic and modernist\n865\ttraditions.\n866\t     The Russian-related dependencies are also historically meaningful. The Russian row shows strong self-persistence, a\n867\trelatively large dependency on the composite Others group, and smaller dependencies on Germany and France. This\n868\tpattern is consistent with the mixed position of Russian music, which developed from national-school traditions while\n869\talso interacting with broader European modernist currents through figures such as Scriabin, Stravinsky, Prokofiev, and\n870\témigré composer networks [8, 31]. Because the Others group combines Czech, Hungarian, Italian, Nordic, American,\n871\tand other repertoires, the Russia–Others dependency should be interpreted as a broad aggregate signal rather than as a\n872\tsingle coherent channel of influence.\n873\t   bFrance,Russia captures the contribution of the Russian trajectory to the next-period French harmonic state. This value\n874\t   𝑊\n875\tis approximately 0.194 in both the DG and FJ matrices, making Russia the largest external source in the French row\n876\tafter France’s own self-dependency (𝑊bFrance,France ≈ 0.629). This is consistent with the strong presence of Russian\n877\tmodernism in Parisian musical life, especially through Diaghilev’s Ballets Russes and the reception of Stravinsky. The\n878\t\fModeling Stylistic Co-evolution in Symbolic Music Heritage Collections                                                  19\n879\t\n880\t\n881\tParis premiere of Le Sacre du printemps in 1913 provides a salient example of how Russian-derived modernist materials\n882\tentered the French cultural environment [31].\n883\t    The Others group has the largest self-weight in the DG matrix. This does not imply that the grouped countries form\n884\ta unified musical tradition. Rather, it reflects the fact that the composite category contains internally heterogeneous\n885\tmaterials whose averaged trajectory is partly stabilized by its own previous state. Its weights are therefore useful for\n886\timproving temporal coverage and detecting broad non-core stylistic movement, but they should not be given the same\n887\tmusicological interpretation as the rows corresponding to Russia, France, Germany, or Austria.\n888\t  The FJ matrix Wb should be interpreted differently from the DG matrix. In DG, W\n889\t                                                                                b is the full transition operator. In FJ,\n890\tW\n891\tb describes the composition of the social component conditional on susceptibility, while the total strength of that\n892\t                                                                                          𝑠 ∗ 𝑖𝑊\n893\t                           S. Thus, the effective contribution of source 𝑗 to target 𝑖 is b\n894\tcomponent is controlled by b                                                                   b ∗ 𝑖 𝑗, not simply 𝑊\n895\t                                                                                                                   b𝑖 𝑗 .\n896\t                                                                    S = (1.0000, 1.0000, 0.9536, 1.0000, 0.8399), in the\n897\t    For the FJ model, the equal-weight susceptibility estimates are b\n898\torder Russia, France, Germany, Austria, and Others. The corresponding stubbornness values, defined as 1 − 𝑠𝑖 , are\n899\t(0, 0, 0.0464, 0, 0.1601). Thus, under equal weighting, the Others group shows the strongest estimated attachment to its\n900\tintrinsic harmonic state, which is consistent with its role as a heterogeneous aggregate.\n901\t   Under PCA-variance weighting, the susceptibility estimates are b  S (VW) = (1.0000, 1.0000, 0.8900, 1.0000, 0.9263). The\n902\tcorresponding stubbornness values are (0, 0, 0.1100, 0, 0.0737). In this specification, the strongest estimated anchoring\n903\tshifts from Others to Germany, suggesting that the leading PCA dimensions emphasize harmonic variance associated\n904\twith the German trajectory.\n905\t    Overall, the estimated matrices suggest three broad historical patterns: strong within-tradition persistence, a close\n906\tGerman–Austrian dependency, and selective cross-dependence among the Russian, French, and broader European\n907\ttrajectories. These patterns are consistent with the historically dense transition from late Romanticism to early\n908\tmodernism between 1875 and 1940.\n909\t\n910\t\n911\t6     Discussion\n912\t6.1    Music-Historical Reading of the Estimated Dynamics\n913\tThe estimated DG and FJ models should be read as compact summaries of harmonic co-evolution, not as direct\n914\tevidence of cultural causation. Several patterns are compatible with established accounts of the late-Romantic and\n915\tearly-modernist transition. Strong self-dependence reflects the persistence of national and institutional traditions across\n916\tfive-year intervals. The close German–Austrian relationship is consistent with their shared Austro-German harmonic,\n917\tpedagogical, and institutional environment. The Russian and French trajectories also support a historically plausible\n918\treading of asymmetric exchange: Russian harmonic practice developed in dialogue with Central European models, while\n919\tRussian musical currents were also present in French modernist culture, particularly through reception networks and\n920\tParis-centered artistic activity. These patterns should be understood as model-based evidence of stylistic co-movement,\n921\tnot as proof of one-way influence among individual composers.\n922\t    This reading is also supported, in a limited diagnostic sense, by the supplementary MIDI stress test reported\n923\tin Appendix C. That experiment uses symbolic MIDI data and BiLSTM era-classifier probabilities rather than the\n924\tChordBERT/PCA harmonic representation used in the main analysis. The resulting influence matrix reproduces\n925\tseveral coarse motifs of the main analysis, including strong within-agent persistence and broadly similar cross-group\n926\tdependency patterns. This qualitative overlap suggests that some high-level features of the estimated dynamics are not\n927\tentirely tied to a single representation pipeline.\n928\t\f20                                                                                                                 He et al.\n929\t\n930\t\n931\t6.2    Aggregation and the “Others” Category\n932\tThe composite “Others” agent is useful for maintaining temporal coverage, but it is not a coherent cultural actor. In\n933\tthis corpus, it groups less densely represented traditions associated with Norway, Finland, Czechia, Italy, Hungary, the\n934\tUnited States, and other contexts. These traditions are not marginal in historical or aesthetic terms; the problem is that\n935\ta single state vector compresses several distinct national, regional, and stylistic trajectories.\n936\t     This aggregation can affect both the estimated influence weights and the FJ anchoring terms. Apparent stubbornness,\n937\tinstability, or anomalous cross-dependencies in the “Others” row may partly reflect internal heterogeneity rather than a\n938\tmeaningful collective stylistic position. This limitation points to an important direction for future work: when sufficient\n939\tdata are available, the aggregate comparison group should be decomposed into finer national, regional, institutional, or\n940\tstylistic agents.\n941\t\n942\t\n943\t6.3    Parsimony and Interpretability\n944\tThe use of a shared influence matrix across PCA dimensions is a deliberate compromise between musical richness and\n945\tmodel interpretability. A separate matrix for each harmonic dimension could capture feature-specific diffusion patterns,\n946\tbut the available historical time series is too short to support such a parameter-rich specification reliably. It would also\n947\tmake the resulting network difficult to interpret as a single cultural dependency structure.\n948\t     The equal-coordinate and PCA-variance-weighted estimators therefore serve complementary roles. The weighted\n949\testimator emphasizes the leading harmonic dimensions and yields modest predictive gains, while the equal-coordinate\n950\testimator provides a transparent robustness check. Because both estimators preserve the same transition equation\n951\tand the same single-matrix interpretation, their agreement supports the stability of the main qualitative conclusions\n952\twithout overstating the precision of the inferred influence network. The MIDI-based stress test extend this robustness\n953\tdiscussion in two complementary directions: the former probes temporal variation in the dependency structure, while\n954\tthe latter tests whether similar coarse patterns can appear in a different symbolic representation space. Together, these\n955\tdiagnostics support the portability of the representation-to-dynamics framework while reinforcing the need for cautious\n956\thistorical interpretation.\n957\t\n958\t\n959\t6.4    Limitations\n960\tSeveral limitations should be emphasized. First, the main DG and FJ specifications use linear transitions and estimate a\n961\tsingle dependency structure over the full analysis window. This choice supports interpretability, but it also compresses\n962\thistorically uneven processes into a long-run average. Events such as World War I, the Russian Revolution, institutional\n963\tdisplacement, and composer migration may have altered the channels of musical exchange in ways that a fixed matrix\n964\tcannot fully represent. historical change.\n965\t     Second, the inferred states are harmonic-style trajectories rather than complete descriptions of musical style. They\n966\tare derived from symbolic chord sequences and contextual chord embeddings, and therefore do not directly capture\n967\trhythm, timbre, orchestration, form, text setting, performance practice, or reception history. The supplementary MIDI\n968\texperiment in Appendix C obtains a broadly similar coarse influence matrix from a different symbolic representation\n969\tspace. Since MIDI-based inputs can preserve rhythmic and temporal aspects that are absent from chord-label sequences,\n970\tthis result suggests that the proposed pipeline is not restricted to the ChordBERT/PCA harmonic representation and\n971\tcan be transferred to richer symbolic music inputs.\n972\t\fModeling Stylistic Co-evolution in Symbolic Music Heritage Collections                                                 21\n973\t\n974\t\n975\t    Third, the country-level trajectories depend on sparse and unevenly distributed historical observations. Causal\n976\tKalman filtering reduces noise and accounts for different annual sample sizes through measurement uncertainty, but it\n977\tcannot recover information from genuinely missing periods. Trajectories based on fewer works remain more uncertain,\n978\tespecially for less densely represented traditions and for boundary regions of the analysis window.\n979\t    Fourth, the PCA projection and component-wise normalization are appropriate for retrospective reconstruction of\n980\tthe 1875–1940 period, but they do not constitute a fully leakage-free forecasting design. A stricter predictive setup\n981\twould fit dimensionality reduction and normalization parameters only on the training period before applying them to\n982\tlater works. The present study should therefore be understood as historical modeling and interpretable retrospective\n983\tanalysis rather than real-time prediction.\n984\t    Finally, the estimated matrices should not be interpreted as direct evidence that one country influenced another.\n985\tThey identify the dependencies that best explain the observed harmonic trajectories under the assumptions of the\n986\tmodel. Historical mechanisms such as pedagogy, migration, publication, performance, institutional affiliation, and\n987\taesthetic opposition require additional archival and musicological evidence. The value of the framework is to provide a\n988\treproducible quantitative layer that can generate, support, or challenge historically grounded interpretations, not to\n989\treplace qualitative music history.\n990\t\n991\t7   Conclusion\n992\tThis article has proposed a representation-to-dynamics framework for studying long-horizon stylistic change in symbolic\n993\tmusical heritage data. By linking chord-based representation learning, temporally causal trajectory reconstruction, and\n994\topinion-dynamics modeling, the framework transforms sparse work-level observations into interpretable group-level\n995\tharmonic trajectories. In this formulation, national or cultural style is not treated as a fixed label, but as an evolving\n996\tstate whose movement can be compared across historical time.\n997\t    The case study on Western art music from 1875–1940 shows that this framework can produce historically readable\n998\tsummaries of harmonic co-evolution. The estimated dynamics capture persistent self-dependence, close German–\n999\tAustrian proximity, and plausible cross-currents among Russian, French, and Central European harmonic idioms. These\n1000\tpatterns are broadly consistent with established accounts of late-Romantic and early-modernist musical exchange, while\n1001\talso showing how sparse observations, heterogeneous aggregation, and model specification choices affect the resulting\n1002\tinfluence-like matrices. The PCA-variance-weighted estimator offers modest predictive gains without changing the\n1003\tinterpretation of a single cross-group dependency structure.\n1004\t    The supplementary diagnostics further clarify the scope of the approach. The MIDI-based stress test shows that the\n1005\tpipeline can be transferred to a different symbolic representation space that includes temporal and rhythmic information\n1006\tbeyond chord labels.\n1007\t    Future work should connect these dynamical summaries more closely to historical mechanisms, including institutions,\n1008\tpedagogy, migration, publication, performance, and reception. It should also extend the representation layer beyond\n1009\tharmony and refine the agent structure beyond coarse national groupings. More broadly, the value of the proposed\n1010\tframework lies in providing a reproducible quantitative layer for cultural heritage research: one that can make\n1011\tassumptions explicit, compare stylistic trajectories across time, and complement, rather than replace, archival and\n1012\tmusicological interpretation.\n1013\t\n1014\tReferences\n1015\t [1] Lowell P. Beveridge. The Christian Scholar, 41(2):175–178, 1958.\n1016\t\f22                                                                                                                                           He et al.\n1017\t\n1018\t\n1019\t [2] N. Cook and A. Pople. The Cambridge History of Twentieth-Century Music. Cambridge histories online. Cambridge University Press, 2004.\n1020\t [3] C. Dahlhaus and J.B. Robinson. Nineteenth-century Music. California studies in 19th century music. University of California Press, 1989.\n1021\t [4] Morris H. DeGroot. Reaching a consensus. Journal of the American Statistical Association, 69(345):118–121, 1974.\n1022\t [5] Jacob Devlin, Ming-Wei Chang, Kenton Lee, and Kristina Toutanova. Bert: Pre-training of deep bidirectional transformers for language understanding,\n1023\t     2019.\n1024\t [6] Noah E. Friedkin and Eugene C. Johnsen. Social influence and opinions. The Journal of Mathematical Sociology, 15(3-4):193–206, 1990.\n1025\t [7] Noah E. Friedkin, Anton V. Proskurnikov, Roberto Tempo, and Sergey E. Parsegov. Network science on belief system dynamics under logic\n1026\t     constraints. Science, 354(6310):321–326, 2016.\n1027\t [8] M. Frolova-Walker. Russian Music and Nationalism: From Glinka to Stalin. Yale University Press, 2007.\n1028\t [9] Tianyu Gao, Xingcheng Yao, and Danqi Chen. Simcse: Simple contrastive learning of sentence embeddings. CoRR, abs/2104.08821, 2021.\n1029\t[10] Mark Gotham, Gianluca Micchi, Néstor Nápoles López, and Malcolm Sailor. When in rome: A meta-corpus of functional harmony. Transactions of\n1030\t     the International Society for Music Information Retrieval, Nov 2023.\n1031\t[11] P. Griffiths. Modern Music and After. Oxford University Press, 2011.\n1032\t[12] Christopher Harte, Mark Sandler, Samer Abdallah, and Emilia Gómez. Symbolic re