{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-bcf3b1cba959eb01", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1200\t          Predicted argmax classes      Modern: 761; Renaissance: 15; Other: 2; Baroque/Classical/Romantic: 0\n1201\t          Five-agent grouping           Russia: 26; France: 70; Germany: 106; Austria: 11; Others: 457\n1202\t          Top-agent grouping            USA: 199; Germany: 106; Belgium: 75; France: 70; England: 59; Italy: 58;\n1203\t                                        Russia: 26; Poland: 25; Others: 52\n1204\t\n1205\t\n1206\t          Table 6. Forecast performance for the MIDI BiLSTM stress test using the full six-dimensional probability vector.\n1207\t\n1208\t\n1209\t                                                                                   RMSE\n1210\t                     Agent design       Model         MAE\n1211\t                                                                  mean          1930        1935         1940\n1212\t                                        DG         0.005967    0.014660     0.006775    0.018722     0.015758\n1213\t                     Five-agent\n1214\t                                        FJ         0.005981    0.014668     0.006777    0.018731     0.015769\n1215\t                                        DG         0.003760    0.010887     0.005148    0.014062     0.011461\n1216\t                     Top-agent\n1217\t                                        FJ         0.003760    0.010806     0.005111    0.014037     0.011275\n1218\t\n1219\t\n1220\tstate space, the MIDI experiment uses six supervised era-classifier probabilities directly as opinion dimensions: Renais-\n1221\tsance, Baroque, Classical, Romantic, Modern, and Other.\n1222\t     Each MIDI file is converted to the model-card feature representation and passed through the BiLSTM classifier.\n1223\tWindow-level logits are averaged within each work and then converted to probabilities by a softmax. Country-level\n1224\tstates are constructed with the same causal Kalman filtering procedure used in the main analysis, using five-year bins,\n1225\t𝑄 = 0.0005, and 𝑅𝑡 = 0.01/𝑁𝑡 .\n1226\t     The main diagnostic finding is that the BiLSTM probability space collapses toward the Modern class. As summarized\n1227\tin Table 5, 761 of the 778 MIDI records have Modern as their predicted argmax class, while Baroque, Classical, and\n1228\tRomantic never appear as the highest-probability class. This concentration may reflect the classifier, the MIDI feature\n1229\treconstruction, or the selected corpus itself. Therefore, this experiment is treated as a stress test rather than as an\n1230\talternative historical opinion space.\n1231\t     Figure 10 visualizes the resulting five-agent Kalman trajectories. The figure confirms that the opinion space is\n1232\tdominated by the Modern dimension, with most other era-probability dimensions remaining close to zero across\n1233\tcountries and time. This motivates our decision to interpret the MIDI experiment as a diagnostic check rather than as a\n1234\tdirect substitute for the ChordBERT/PCA representation used in the main analysis.\n1235\t     We evaluate both the original five-agent design and a MIDI-specific top-agent design. The five-agent design preserves\n1236\tcomparability with the main experiment, whereas the top-agent design better reflects the country distribution of\n1237\tthe MIDI corpus. Models are fitted on 1875–1925 and recursively evaluated on 1930, 1935, and 1940. Table 6 reports\n1238\tperformance using the full six-dimensional probability vector.\n1239\t     Although the absolute RMSE values are small, the fitted opinion-dynamics models do not outperform a persistence\n1240\tbaseline. For the five-agent design, the RMSE skill values are −0.124207 for DG and −0.124845 for FJ. For the top-agent\n1241\t\fModeling Stylistic Co-evolution in Symbolic Music Heritage Collections                                                       27\n1242\t\n1243\t\n1244\t\n1245\t\n1246\tFig. 10. Five-agent causal Kalman trajectories in the six-dimensional BiLSTM era-probability opinion space. The Modern dimension\n1247\tdominates the trajectories, while most other era dimensions remain close to zero.\n1248\t\n1249\t\n1250\tdesign, the corresponding values are −0.176693 and −0.167879. Thus, the high apparent trajectory similarity mainly\n1251\treflects the near-constant structure induced by the collapsed classifier-probability space.\n1252\t  For completeness, Figure 11 reports the estimated DG influence matrices for both the five-agent and top-agent\n1253\tdesigns. These matrices are included to show that the estimation procedure remains numerically well-defined under the\n1254\tMIDI BiLSTM representation. However, because the underlying opinion space is dominated by the Modern class and\n1255\tthe forecasts do not improve over persistence, we do not interpret these matrices as historical evidence of cross-national\n1256\tmusical influence.\n1257\t  The MIDI stress test has two implications. First, the proposed state-construction and dynamics-estimation procedure\n1258\tcan be applied to a separate symbolic-MIDI corpus and to a different representation space. Second, supervised era-\n1259\tclassifier probabilities are not a suitable replacement for the ChordBERT/PCA harmonic representation used in the\n1260\tmain analysis. Because the BiLSTM opinion vectors collapse toward one dominant class and fail to improve over\n1261\tpersistence, we treat the MIDI experiment as a diagnostic stress test rather than as evidence for the main historical\n1262\tinfluence estimates.\n1263\t\f28                                                                                                                    He et al.\n1264\t\n1265\t\n1266\t\n1267\t\n1268\t                 (a) Five-agent DG matrix                                            (b) Top-agent DG matrix\n1269\t\n1270\tFig. 11. Estimated DG influence matrices in the MIDI BiLSTM stress test. Rows denote target agents and columns denote source\n1271\tagents. Because the underlying opinion space is dominated by the Modern class, these matrices are reported as diagnostic outputs\n1272\trather than as historical cross-national influence estimates.\n1273\t\f\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "46adb53f-db2a-48fc-9bfc-1fd2b9d177eb", "uuid": "7228d713-2a5a-4f04-9540-c5eb5e60ae3c", "timestamp": "2026-07-28T06:23:37.688Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_2607_23957.txt", "content": "          Predicted argmax classes      Modern: 761; Renaissance: 15; Other: 2; Baroque/Classical/Romantic: 0\n          Five-agent grouping           Russia: 26; France: 70; Germany: 106; Austria: 11; Others: 457\n          Top-agent grouping            USA: 199; Germany: 106; Belgium: 75; France: 70; England: 59; Italy: 58;\n                                        Russia: 26; Poland: 25; Others: 52\n\n\n          Table 6. Forecast performance for the MIDI BiLSTM stress test using the full six-dimensional probability vector.\n\n\n                                                                                   RMSE\n                     Agent design       Model         MAE\n                                                                  mean          1930        1935         1940\n                                        DG         0.005967    0.014660     0.006775    0.018722     0.015758\n                     Five-agent\n                                        FJ         0.005981    0.014668     0.006777    0.018731     0.015769\n                                        DG         0.003760    0.010887     0.005148    0.014062     0.011461\n                     Top-agent\n                                        FJ         0.003760    0.010806     0.005111    0.014037     0.011275\n\n\nstate space, the MIDI experiment uses six supervised era-classifier probabilities directly as opinion dimensions: Renais-\nsance, Baroque, Classical, Romantic, Modern, and Other.\n     Each MIDI file is converted to the model-card feature representation and passed through the BiLSTM classifier.\nWindow-level logits are averaged within each work and then converted to probabilities by a softmax. Country-level\nstates are constructed with the same causal Kalman filtering procedure used in the main analysis, using five-year bins,\n𝑄 = 0.0005, and 𝑅𝑡 = 0.01/𝑁𝑡 .\n     The main diagnostic finding is that the BiLSTM probability space collapses toward the Modern class. As summarized\nin Table 5, 761 of the 778 MIDI records have Modern as their predicted argmax class, while Baroque, Classical, and\nRomantic never appear as the highest-probability class. This concentration may reflect the classifier, the MIDI feature\nreconstruction, or the selected corpus itself. Therefore, this experiment is treated as a stress test rather than as an\nalternative historical opinion space.\n     Figure 10 visualizes the resulting five-agent Kalman trajectories. The figure confirms that the opinion space is\ndominated by the Modern dimension, with most other era-probability dimensions remaining close to zero across\ncountries and time. This motivates our decision to interpret the MIDI experiment as a diagnostic check rather than as a\ndirect substitute for the ChordBERT/PCA representation used in the main analysis.\n     We evaluate both the original five-agent design and a MIDI-specific top-agent design. The five-agent design preserves\ncomparability with the main experiment, whereas the top-agent design better reflects the country distribution of\nthe MIDI corpus. Models are fitted on 1875–1925 and recursively evaluated on 1930, 1935, and 1940. Table 6 reports\nperformance using the full six-dimensional probability vector.\n     Although the absolute RMSE values are small, the fitted opinion-dynamics models do not outperform a persistence\nbaseline. For the five-agent design, the RMSE skill values are −0.124207 for DG and −0.124845 for FJ. For the top-agent\n\fModeling Stylistic Co-evolution in Symbolic Music Heritage Collections                                                       27\n\n\n\n\nFig. 10. Five-agent causal Kalman trajectories in the six-dimensional BiLSTM era-probability opinion space. The Modern dimension\ndominates the trajectories, while most other era dimensions remain close to zero.\n\n\ndesign, the corresponding values are −0.176693 and −0.167879. Thus, the high apparent trajectory similarity mainly\nreflects the near-constant structure induced by the collapsed classifier-probability space.\n  For completeness, Figure 11 reports the estimated DG influence matrices for both the five-agent and top-agent\ndesigns. These matrices are included to show that the estimation procedure remains numerically well-defined under the\nMIDI BiLSTM representation. However, because the underlying opinion space is dominated by the Modern class and\nthe forecasts do not improve over persistence, we do not interpret these matrices as historical evidence of cross-national\nmusical influence.\n  The MIDI stress test has two implications. First, the proposed state-construction and dynamics-estimation procedure\ncan be applied to a separate symbolic-MIDI corpus and to a different representation space. Second, supervised era-\nclassifier probabilities are not a suitable replacement for the ChordBERT/PCA harmonic representation used in the\nmain analysis. Because the BiLSTM opinion vectors collapse toward one dominant class and fail to improve over\npersistence, we treat the MIDI experiment as a diagnostic stress test rather than as evidence for the main historical\ninfluence estimates.\n\f28                                                                                                                    He et al.\n\n\n\n\n                 (a) Five-agent DG matrix                                            (b) Top-agent DG matrix\n\nFig. 11. Estimated DG influence matrices in the MIDI BiLSTM stress test. Rows denote target agents and columns denote source\nagents. Because the underlying opinion space is dominated by the Modern class, these matrices are reported as diagnostic outputs\nrather than as historical cross-national influence estimates.\n\f", "numLines": 74, "startLine": 1200, "totalLines": 1273}}}
